window.LING406_TRANSCRIPTS_V4 = {"revision":"ling406-v4-source-wording","scripts":{"speech1-01":[],"speech1-02":[{"text":"**Speech perception** lets us take this mess and retrieve a message, even though the acoustic signal changes continuously and the same message can come from very different physical stimuli. Producing “I can see you” involves overlapping changes in the vocal tract, and the sound contains changing concentrations of energy rather than a separate, isolated sound for each written letter.","children":[]},{"text":"Different speakers can produce the same message with very different physical stimuli. The frequencies associated with a vowel vary across speakers, and productions of different vowels can overlap. A listener nevertheless recognizes the words across those physical differences.","children":[]}],"speech1-03":[{"text":"Somehow we have to turn a **highly variable, analog signal into something more abstract**, so that the language system can operate on it. An analog signal varies continuously, while a symbolic representation identifies an abstract unit that the language system can use. Small changes in frequency, timing, or intensity do not always create a new phoneme or a different word.","children":[]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** is a proposed translation from physical to symbolic information. Physically different sounds can be treated as members of one category, while a similar physical change at a category boundary produces a different identity. The resulting category is more stable than the exact acoustic pattern that produced it.","children":[]}],"speech1-04":[{"text":"The shape of the **vocal tract** affects the **amplification of particular resonant frequencies**, called formants, so different tract shapes emphasize different parts of the sound. Changing the position of the tongue or the opening of the mouth changes which frequencies receive the strongest amplification.","children":[]},{"text":"The vowels in heed, hid, head, had, hod, hawed, hood, and who’d involve different vocal-tract shapes and different patterns of resonance. A vowel’s identity depends on the combination of these resonances, rather than one frequency that remains identical for every vowel.","children":[]},{"text":"A **formant transition** is a change in a formant frequency over time. It corresponds to a changing configuration of the vocal tract as a speaker moves into or out of a sound.","children":[]}],"speech1-05":[{"text":"The **English vowels** in bead, bid, bade, bed, bad, bod, baud, bode, “bood,” and booed differ in their acoustic energy across time and frequency, which a **spectrogram** separates into visible patterns. Time is measured in milliseconds, frequency in hertz, and darker regions contain more energy. The prominent horizontal energy bands include the vowel’s formants.","children":[]},{"text":"The vowels in **bead, bid, bade, bed, bad, bod, baud, bode, “bood,” and booed** have different combinations of first and second formant frequencies. Bead has a relatively low first formant and high second formant, while vowels closer to bad have a higher first formant. The rounded back vowels near booed have a much lower second formant.","children":[]},{"text":"The first formant, **F1**, is the lowest resonance, and the second formant, **F2**, is the next higher resonance. Their relationship distinguishes vowels even when the surrounding consonants are similar.","children":[]}],"speech1-06":[{"text":"In **source-filter synthesis**, the vowel **/u/** has its first formant at about **300 Hz** and its second formant very low, around **900 Hz**, so the two lowest resonances are concentrated in the lower frequencies. Both frequencies belong to the resonance pattern within the same vowel, rather than representing two separate spoken sounds.","children":[]},{"text":"The waveform records pressure variation over time, while the spectrogram separates the same recording into its frequency components. The low F1 and F2 are part of the resonance pattern associated with this /u/ production.","children":[]},{"text":"**Praat** is speech-analysis software that can estimate these frequencies. Those estimates allow the source of the voice and the filtering contribution of the vocal tract to be manipulated separately.","children":[]}],"speech1-07":[{"text":"**Praat can automatically find formants**, and the analysis identifies five resonances in this /u/ recording without requiring each resonance to be located manually. Each estimate follows a resonant frequency as it changes during the sound. **F1, F2, and F3** are usually the formants most relevant to phonemic processing, although the manipulation here concerns F1 and F2.","children":[]},{"text":"The numbers specify frequency order, with F1 identifying the lowest formant and F2 identifying the second lowest resonance within the same sound. The /u/ production retains a low F1 near 300 Hz and a low F2 near 900 Hz while higher resonances occur above them.","children":[]}],"speech1-08":[{"text":"Extracting the **source from the sound** uses a **model of the filter based on the formant frequencies**, separating the voice’s excitation from the amplification imposed by the vocal tract. The vocal tract supplies the resonant filtering, and its formant frequencies can be used to estimate that contribution.","children":[]},{"text":"Removing the estimated filter leaves an approximately **unfiltered source**. The result sounds more like a buzz than the original vowel because the particular pattern of vocal-tract amplification has been removed.","children":[]},{"text":"The source continues to supply acoustic energy, and applying a filter to that same source can restore a vowel-like resonance pattern. Excitation and resonance can therefore be varied separately.","children":[]}],"speech1-09":[{"text":"The original filter is the **first four formants from the original recording**, preserving the estimated pattern of resonance against which the altered filters can be compared. Applying it to the extracted source restores approximately the original resonance pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"A second filter raises **F1** slightly while leaving the other formants approximately unchanged. A third filter raises **F2** substantially instead. Raising F1 changes the lowest resonance, while raising F2 changes the second resonance in that frequency pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"Across the three manipulations, the **source remains constant**, preserving the same underlying excitation while the applied filter changes. Differences in the resulting vowel quality therefore arise from the altered filtering rather than from recording a different speaker or a new vocal source.","children":[]}],"speech1-10":[{"text":"**Multiplying the source by the filters gives us new vowels**, because the same source receives different patterns of frequency amplification depending on which filter is applied. Applying the filter with raised F1 increases the frequency of the lowest strong resonance, while applying the filter with raised F2 increases the second resonance instead.","children":[]},{"text":"**Source–filter synthesis** produces different vowel qualities from the same source by changing the resonances that amplify it. The resulting sound depends on both the energy supplied by the source and the frequency pattern imposed by the filter.","children":[]}],"speech1-11":[{"text":"**Formant values can provide a pretty good description of vowel space**, meaning that vowels occupy different regions defined by their combinations of resonant frequencies. F1 and F2 provide useful coordinates because different vowel qualities are associated with different combinations of these resonances.","children":[]},{"text":"A vowel is distributed across a range of values rather than located at one exact point. Anatomical differences between speakers, speaking rate, and the sounds before and after the vowel all change its acoustic realization. These changes can produce overlap between the formant values of different vowels.","children":[]},{"text":"Formant values describe vowels especially well for a particular talker speaking carefully. Holding other properties constant, manipulating steady-state formants can produce recognizable approximations of different vowels, even though natural speech includes variation over time.","children":[]}],"speech1-12":[{"text":"The **pattern playback synthesizer** converts a painted spectrographic pattern into sound, allowing controlled speech-like stimuli to be produced from a specified acoustic design. The pattern specifies how energy at different frequencies changes over time, so moving a painted band changes the frequency pattern produced.","children":[]},{"text":"Light from a source passes through a cylindrical lens and a rotating **tone wheel**. A lens and a 45-degree mirror direct the modulated light toward the spectrogram pattern. Different parts of the pattern transmit or reflect different amounts of light.","children":[]},{"text":"A **light collector and photocell** convert the resulting light fluctuations into an electrical signal. An amplifier increases the signal and a loudspeaker produces the sound. Changing the pattern therefore changes the acoustic output without requiring a speaker to produce a new utterance.","children":[]}],"speech1-13":[{"text":"**Stop consonants are distinguished by formant transitions**, because the audible part of **/b/, /d/, or /g/** occurs while the jaw is opening and the vocal tract is changing shape. Their audible release occurs as the jaw opens and the vocal tract moves into the following vowel, so the early resonance changes contain information about the consonant.","children":[]},{"text":"In the synthetic syllables used by **Liberman and colleagues (1957)**, the lower formant remains similar while the upper formant begins at different frequencies. Its transition can rise into the vowel, remain relatively flat, or fall into the same steady-state region.","children":[]},{"text":"The pattern playback synthesizer produced speech-like sounds with these controlled transitions. Altering the onset of the upper formant changes the consonantal information while preserving much of the following vowel.","children":[]}],"speech1-14":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** varies a controlled acoustic property across a sequence of stimuli, allowing equally sized physical changes to be compared with listeners’ labeling and discrimination responses. F1 is held constant while the onset frequency of F2 changes linearly, so each adjacent pair differs by the same programmed amount.","children":[]},{"text":"At one end, F2 begins below its steady-state value and rises into the vowel. As the onset frequency increases, the transition becomes flatter and eventually falls from above the steady-state value. The stimuli remain evenly spaced acoustically even if listeners assign them different phoneme labels.","children":[]},{"text":"Equal spacing allows a within-category pair to be compared with a boundary-crossing pair of the same physical separation. A difference in discrimination between those pairs concerns perceptual organization rather than a larger programmed frequency change.","children":[]}],"speech1-15":[{"text":"In a simple **ideal observer** model assuming perception is **non-categorical**, categorization would get **“blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**, with neighboring categories becoming increasingly plausible. The endpoints correspond to /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","children":[]},{"text":"A prototype is a clear representative of a category. Moving away from it makes the stimulus progressively less characteristic of that category and more characteristic of a neighboring category. Under this prediction, stimulus 2 would receive about **75% /b/** and **25% /d/** responses.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling remains a choice among categories, but uncertainty changes smoothly between them. The predicted /b/ response declines gradually as /d/ responses increase, followed by a gradual exchange between /d/ and /g/ responses.","children":[]}],"speech1-16":[{"text":"In an ideal observer model assuming perception is **linear**, discrimination performance should be **independent of labeling**, so equally separated F2-onset values should be equally discriminable across the continuum. A two-step pair such as 1 versus 3 has the same acoustic separation as 5 versus 7.","children":[]},{"text":"In a **labeling task**, a listener assigns a category such as /b/, /d/, or /g/. In a **discrimination task**, the listener judges whether two stimuli are the same or different. These tasks ask for different responses even when they use the same acoustic continuum.","children":[]},{"text":"The linear account predicts approximately constant discrimination accuracy across equal-distance pairs. Labeling probabilities can still rise and fall across prototypes without producing corresponding peaks in discrimination.","children":[]}],"speech1-17":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** in **Liberman and colleagues’ results** includes abrupt changes in phoneme labeling, although the stimuli themselves progress through evenly spaced acoustic changes. Early stimuli receive mainly /b/ responses, middle stimuli mainly /d/ responses, and late stimuli mainly /g/ responses, even though F2 onset changes in equal acoustic steps.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination is better near the /b/–/d/ and /d/–/g/ boundaries than within the stretches receiving one label. The one-step, two-step, and three-step comparisons all relate sensitivity to the location of those boundaries. Chance performance is about **50% correct** in these discrimination tasks.","children":[]},{"text":"Wider physical separation generally improves performance, but the category relationship also matters. Sounds assigned to different phonemes are easier to distinguish than similarly spaced sounds assigned to the same phoneme.","children":[]},{"text":"Predictions based on the labeling responses capture the boundary-related peaks in discrimination. Listeners retain some acoustic sensitivity beyond the simplest categorical prediction, so identification and discrimination are closely related without being identical measures.","children":[]}],"speech1-18":[],"speech1-19":[{"text":"For **beach–peach identification**, one listener’s responses stay with one category through steps 1–5, become mixed around step 6, and switch to the other category by step 7, locating the labeling boundary near the middle of the continuum. The transition is abrupt despite the equal acoustic steps.","children":[]},{"text":"The corresponding discrimination responses are strongest for pairs **4–6 and 5–7**, which include the category transition, and weak for pairs contained well inside one category. The pair 6–8 is intermediate because step 6 itself receives mixed labels.","children":[]},{"text":"Another listener has a later identification boundary, around steps 6–7, and stronger discrimination for pairs toward that region. Category boundaries can vary between individuals while the relationship between labeling and discrimination remains similar.","children":[]}],"speech1-20":[{"text":"**Not everyone has exactly the same category boundary**, so the point where a listener switches from one phoneme label to another can differ across people hearing the same continuum. One person may switch from beach to peach at an earlier continuum position than another person, so the most discriminable pair can also occur at a different position.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination is generally best for stimuli perceived as belonging to different categories. Equal physical differences within one category can be difficult to hear because both sounds are treated as the same phoneme.","children":[]},{"text":"The shortened classroom discrimination procedure included only **different trials**. Every pair was physically different, so “different” was the correct physical judgment on every trial. A “same” response nevertheless occurred when the listener did not detect that difference.","children":[]}],"speech1-21":[{"text":"The **bear–pear identification** responses contain a relatively clear switch between labels, while **bear–pear discrimination** varies irregularly across the tested stimulus pairs rather than following a perfectly smooth pattern. The **beach–peach** responses also contain fluctuations rather than a smooth progression across stimulus pairs.","children":[]},{"text":"Identification and discrimination are separate behavioral measurements, and the responses can vary across trials and listeners. An irregular measured response at one continuum position is not an additional phoneme category. The category comparison concerns the relationship between the range of assigned labels and the discrimination of the corresponding pairs.","children":[]}],"speech1-22":[{"text":"**Being worse at hearing some differences between sounds** can help recognition when those differences occur between acoustically varying productions of the same phoneme or word. A system that treated every small physical difference as a change in linguistic identity would separate tokens that belong to the same phoneme or word.","children":[]},{"text":"**Categorical perception** reduces sensitivity to some within-category differences while preserving distinctions between categories. Several varying acoustic inputs can therefore provide the same symbolic input to word recognition. The reduced sensitivity concerns variation that does not require a different linguistic identity.","children":[]}],"speech1-23":[],"speech1-24":[{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, an infant learns to turn toward the sound source when an auditory change is associated with a visual reward, providing a behavioral measure of discrimination. The response makes a detected sound difference measurable without requiring the infant to speak.","children":[]},{"text":"In **high-amplitude sucking**, sucking behavior provides the response measure. Repeated exposure to a sound can reduce responding, and an increase after a sound change indicates renewed interest in a difference the infant detects. The two procedures use different infant behaviors to measure auditory discrimination.","children":[]}],"speech1-25":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** can be measured through sucking responses, as in **Eimas and colleagues (1971)**, where young infants heard repeated synthetic syllables before a controlled change in the sound. As responding declined during repeated exposure, the stimulus either changed across an adult phoneme boundary, changed within one adult category, or remained unchanged.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **20D** condition, the VOT difference was **20 milliseconds** and the stimuli belonged to different adult categories. The tested values were **+20 and +40 milliseconds**. Sucking increased again when the stimulus changed across the category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **20S** condition, the VOT difference was also 20 milliseconds, but both stimuli belonged to the same adult category. The values were **−20 and 0**, or **+60 and +80 milliseconds**. Sucking did not recover in the same way.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **0** control condition, no stimulus change occurred, and sucking continued to decline. The stronger recovery for the equally sized boundary-crossing change indicates category-related discrimination before infants can produce word labels.","children":[]}],"speech1-26":[{"text":"**Perceptual narrowing for speech categories** describes the developmental reduction in sensitivity to speech distinctions that are not maintained by experience with the surrounding language. English-learning infants discriminate the tested **Hindi** and **Salish** contrasts more successfully early in infancy than near the end of the first year.","children":[]},{"text":"At **6–8 months**, many English-learning infants reach the discrimination criterion for both contrasts. Performance declines at **8–10 months** and is much lower at **10–12 months**. Infants with relevant Hindi or Salish language experience retain much stronger discrimination.","children":[]},{"text":"**Werker and Tees** therefore relate the developmental change to language experience. Growing sensitivity to a native-language system can coincide with reduced sensitivity to contrasts outside that system.","children":[]}],"speech1-27":[{"text":"Under one account, **speech perception depends on categorical perception abilities that are a special property of the language system**, rather than simply depending on a general response to sound. These abilities are claimed to be available very early in development.","children":[]},{"text":"Under the **“citizens of the world”** account, children initially hear contrasts across languages and then rapidly tune perception to their own language. Development mainly selects which early distinctions remain useful, producing a narrower system adapted to the surrounding speech.","children":[]}],"speech1-28":[{"text":"**Categorical perception is not limited to language** and is **not limited to humans**, so it can reflect a general way of organizing sound rather than a mechanism operating exclusively on human phonemes. Similar category-related behavior can occur for nonspeech sounds and in animals without human language. The behavior is also **task-dependent**, because the response demanded by an experiment influences how categorical performance appears.","children":[]},{"text":"**Infant speech perception is not completely universal.** Some contrasts are more detectable than others at the same early age. Development therefore involves differences in starting sensitivity as well as changes associated with language experience.","children":[]}],"speech1-29":[{"text":"**Categorical perception of non-speech sounds** occurs in **Cutting and Rosner’s experiment (1974)**, which varies how quickly an acoustic signal increases in amplitude, its **rise time**. Rise time is how quickly a sound reaches its stronger amplitude after it begins. Short-rise-time sounds were heard as more **plucked**, and longer-rise-time sounds as more **bowed**.","children":[]},{"text":"The materials included sawtooth waves and sine waves, with speech syllables used for comparison. A rapid onset and a gradual onset alter an auditory property without requiring a phoneme contrast.","children":[]},{"text":"For sawtooth waves, pluck responses decreased and bow responses increased across rise times from about **0 to 80 milliseconds**. Discrimination was strongest near the labeling transition, around the middle of that range, rather than constant across equal rise-time differences. Categorical organization can therefore occur in nonspeech hearing.","children":[]}],"speech1-30":[{"text":"**Categorical perception of non-speech sounds by infants** was tested by **Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)** using sucking responses to measure whether infants detected a change in the sound. The conditions were **no change**, a **within-category** change, and a **between-category** change.","children":[]},{"text":"Sucking declined with repeated exposure in all three conditions. Responding remained low after no change and after a within-category change, but increased after a between-category change before declining again.","children":[]},{"text":"The infants were more responsive to a nonspeech change that crossed the perceptual boundary. Early category-related discrimination therefore occurs beyond speech stimuli.","children":[]}],"speech1-31":[{"text":"In **categorical perception of speech sounds by chinchillas**, animals were **trained using operant conditioning** to associate a response to one end of a speech continuum with food. Either a **0-millisecond-lag** or **80-millisecond-lag** stimulus served as an **SD**, a discriminative stimulus signaling that the response would be rewarded.","children":[]},{"text":"Intermediate VOT values tested how the learned response generalized across a **/ga/–/ka/** continuum. Responses changed sharply across a limited range of VOTs in both chinchillas and human comparison listeners.","children":[]},{"text":"Increasing the intensity of the initial frication burst and aspiration noise could shift the boundary toward a shorter VOT or change the steepness of the response curve. Acoustic properties influenced categorization in a nonhuman animal as well as in humans. The animal’s responses did not require possession of human language.","children":[]}],"speech1-32":[{"text":"**Some contrasts are less salient than others for infants**, with English-learning infants at **4–5 months** responding more clearly to changes in the native **[ma]–[na]** contrast than to the nonnative **[na]–[ŋa]** contrast. Longer looking after a change indicates that the infant detected a difference from repeated same trials.","children":[]},{"text":"**[ma]** begins with a bilabial nasal, **[na]** with an alveolar nasal, and **[ŋa]** with a velar nasal. The contrasts therefore differ in which nasal places of articulation have to be separated.","children":[]},{"text":"Among Filipino-learning infants, the native **[na]–[ŋa]** distinction produces little same-versus-change difference at **6–8 months**, but a clearer difference at **10–12 months**. Experience can improve a distinction that was initially weak, rather than only preserve distinctions that were already clear.","children":[]}],"speech2-01":[],"speech2-02":[{"text":"In **Klatt’s model of speech perception (1979)**, the analog signal comes in through the **cochlea**, and the **peripheral auditory system** performs a spectral analysis of the speech waveform. This separates the signal into its frequency components. **Acoustic property detectors** use that information and produce changing detector outputs over time.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonetic feature detectors** convert those outputs into probabilities for features such as voicing. **Segmental analysis** organizes the features into a segmental feature matrix, a representation of the sequence of speech segments. **Lexical search** uses that sequence to activate candidate words, called lexical hypotheses.","children":[]},{"text":"The strict staged account assumes that word identification follows identification of the constituent phonemes. Some acoustic information is lost when a variable analog signal becomes a set of categories, allowing different physical tokens to activate the same linguistic unit.","children":[]},{"text":"**Cascaded processing** allows partial information to reach the next level before the current level has finished. **Feedback** allows information from a higher level, such as words, to influence a lower level, such as phonemes. These properties differ from a sequence that passes completed outputs only in one direction.","children":[]}],"speech2-03":[{"text":"A **voicing continuum** between **peach** and **beach** varies **voice onset time**, the delay between releasing the stop consonant and starting vocal-fold vibration at the beginning of each spoken word. A longer delay supports the voiceless /p/ in peach, while a shorter delay supports the voiced /b/ in beach. Much of the remaining word stays acoustically similar.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **identification responses**, changes along the voicing continuum lead to an abrupt switch between the beach and peach categories. Steps 1–5 receive one label, step 6 receives mixed labels, and steps 7–9 receive the other label. Step 6 is acoustically and perceptually close to the category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination is strongest for equally separated pairs near that transition, especially 4–6 and 5–7. Pairs well inside one category are harder to distinguish even though their physical separation is the same.","children":[]}],"speech2-04":[{"text":"In a **threshold model**, **activity is only passed forward from one level to the next when an activity threshold is crossed**, so a higher level waits until the lower level reaches that requirement. The lower level can change without producing any activity at the level above it.","children":[]},{"text":"Feature activation rises and falls between the thresholds for **+voiced** and **−voiced**. Early fluctuations remain below either threshold, so phoneme activation stays at zero. Only the final rise crosses the +voiced threshold, producing an abrupt increase in activation for **/b/**.","children":[]},{"text":"Categorical perception therefore arises at phoneme identification in this account. Later word recognition receives the selected phoneme, rather than all the graded feature differences that occurred before the threshold was reached.","children":[]}],"speech2-05":[{"text":"In a **cascaded model**, **activity is passed forward continuously over time from one level to the next**, allowing incomplete and still-changing information to begin influencing higher levels. The phoneme level responds while the feature level is still changing instead of waiting for a fixed threshold.","children":[]},{"text":"Increasing evidence for voicing gradually increases support for **/b/**, while evidence against voicing favors **/p/**. Partial changes in the feature representation remain available to the next level.","children":[]},{"text":"A categorical final response can emerge later in processing or from the response demanded by the task. A listener who must select beach or peach gives one answer even if the recognition process contains graded uncertainty before that decision.","children":[]}],"speech2-06":[{"text":"In **making furniture**, a **lumber mill** receives **trees** as input and produces **lumber**, transforming the material into a form that a furniture factory can accept. A **furniture factory** takes that lumber and produces unassembled furniture. An **IKEA customer** takes the unassembled parts and produces usable furniture.","children":[]},{"text":"Each operation accepts **a specific transformed product**, so its required input is defined by what the preceding operation has already produced. The furniture factory cannot use an unprocessed tree in place of lumber, and the customer cannot assemble a finished chair directly from a tree. In **thresholded processing**, a level waits until processing at the preceding level has produced the form of input it requires.","children":[]}],"speech2-07":[{"text":"In **thresholded activity**, each layer can only take a **specific input that has been transformed completely by the previous layer**, so processing waits for the required transformation to finish. Its output is a finished representation in the form the next stage can use.","children":[]},{"text":"Word recognition would therefore wait for a phoneme output in the same way that furniture manufacturing waits for a shipment of lumber. Partial acoustic uncertainty would not continuously influence words while phoneme identification remained unfinished.","children":[]}],"speech2-08":[{"text":"In a **food web**, levels **continuously interact with one another**, so a change in the health or abundance of one population affects the organisms that depend on it while those populations are still changing. A drought reduces desert plants, affecting kangaroo rats and then the snakes that depend on those animals. The downstream populations respond while the upstream changes are still occurring.","children":[]},{"text":"**Cascaded processing** likewise allows a changing state at one level to influence another without waiting for a completed endpoint. Partial feature activity can affect phonemes, and partial phoneme activity can affect words.","children":[]},{"text":"Food-web relationships also include reciprocal influences between populations, because changes in one population affect organisms linked to it. Hawks, snakes, lizards, rats, insects, scorpions, and desert foxes participate in connected feeding relationships. **Feedback** means that a change higher in the network can alter activity at an earlier level, rather than influence traveling only in one direction.","children":[]}],"speech2-09":[{"text":"Phoneme identification for word recognition could be more like **thresholded lumber-mill processing** or **cascaded food-web processing**, depending on whether lexical candidates wait for a completed phoneme decision. A thresholded account transmits a completed category, while a cascaded account transmits developing evidence.","children":[]},{"text":"They can produce the same final word choice while predicting different processing before the choice. If two sounds both receive a /b/ label but differ in their acoustic ambiguity, a cascaded system can maintain different amounts of support for the competing /p/ word.","children":[]}],"speech2-10":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin, and Spivey (2003)** tested **identification of a /b/–/p/ continuum in a visual world display for eye-tracking**, measuring lexical competition while participants decided which object matched the spoken input. Varying VOT changed the evidence for the initial phoneme while the alternative word interpretations were represented by pictures.","children":[]},{"text":"A display contained a target, a phonological competitor, and two unrelated fillers. **Bear** and **pear** differed only in the initial phoneme, while a **lamp** and a **ship** served as fillers. Participants heard a token and selected the picture that best matched it.","children":[]},{"text":"Eye movements measured which candidate attracted a look before the final selection. A look toward pear during a bear response indicates that the losing word participated in recognition even though the participant eventually selected bear.","children":[]}],"speech2-11":[{"text":"Participants **used the mouse to select the picture most consistent with what they heard**, choosing **bear** or **pear** according to their final interpretation of the initial consonant. The proportion of pear choices increased sharply as VOT moved from the /b/ end to the /p/ end, with a category boundary near **17.5 milliseconds**.","children":[]},{"text":"The final choices formed a typical step-like identification function. Several physically distinct tokens on the /b/ side produced the same bear response, and several tokens on the /p/ side produced the same pear response. The click records which candidate won, rather than every candidate that was active before it.","children":[]}],"speech2-12":[{"text":"**Ambiguity is gradient**, because there are **more looks to the competitor close to the boundary**, even on trials that end with the same final identification response. On trials ending with a bear response, the pear picture attracted more looking as VOT approached the boundary from the /b/ side. On trials ending with pear, bear attracted more looking near the /p/ side of the boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"Competitor fixations rose after word onset and then declined as the target interpretation became established. More ambiguous tokens kept the competing object involved for longer.","children":[]},{"text":"Final identification was categorical, but the processing that preceded it was **gradient**. Sounds receiving the same final word label still differed in how strongly and how long they activated the competing word.","children":[]}],"speech2-13":[{"text":"When **competitor looking** is aligned to each listener’s **category boundary**, the graded effect occurs within bear responses and within pear responses rather than depending only on a change in the final label. Among bear responses, increasing VOT toward the boundary increases looks to pear. Among pear responses, moving toward that boundary increases looks to bear.","children":[]},{"text":"The early interval was **300–1100 milliseconds** after onset and the late interval **1100–1900 milliseconds**. Competitor looking remained greater near the boundary, including in the later interval. Uncertainty therefore persisted in the duration of competition rather than disappearing as soon as a token supported one final category.","children":[]}],"speech2-14":[{"text":"**Activation flows in a cascaded way from features to phonemes**, allowing acoustic evidence to influence phoneme and word candidates while the speech signal is still arriving. A sound identified as /b/ on every trial can nevertheless vary in how strongly it activates the **voiced** feature.","children":[]},{"text":"A stimulus can clearly be **bilabial** and a **stop** while its voicing remains uncertain. Bilabial means produced with both lips, and a stop involves a closure followed by release. Those shared properties support both /b/ and /p/, leaving voicing to distinguish them.","children":[]},{"text":"The remaining uncertainty activates both lexical candidates to different degrees. More support for the losing phoneme creates more support for its word, increasing looks to the competing picture while the participant decides where to click.","children":[]},{"text":"**TRACE** represents features, phonemes, and words at different positions in time. Repeated units allow different candidates to become active at different possible locations within the unfolding input.","children":[]}],"speech2-15":[{"text":"**Partial activation can reach all the way to the lexical level**, because **Andruski and colleagues (1994)** obtained **semantic priming with nonwords** that partly matched real spoken words. Hearing **beace** facilitated a response to **war**, while hearing **ging** facilitated a response to **queen**. Beace partially activates peace, whose meaning is related to war, and ging partially activates king, whose meaning is related to queen.","children":[]},{"text":"Semantic priming means that a preceding item facilitates processing of a meaning-related target. The effect indicates that partial sound information can reach lexical representations and influence meaning even when the input is not a correctly formed word.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparison also included ordinary word primes, such as **bill** before **drug**, and unrelated primes. Related inputs produced faster responses than unrelated inputs, while altering the prime’s initial sound could weaken the benefit. The amount of matching phonetic information affected lexical activation rather than simply allowing or blocking it altogether.","children":[]}],"speech2-16":[{"text":"The **number of looks to the other member of the minimal pair increases gradually as you approach the category boundary**, even when those stimuli receive the same final phoneme label. A **cascaded account** preserves the partial evidence that varies across those tokens and passes it into word recognition.","children":[]},{"text":"A strict threshold account discards those distinctions when every token produces the same completed phoneme output. The later word level would then receive the same information and lack the changing input needed to explain the graded competitor effect.","children":[]}],"speech2-17":[{"text":"**Feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer** allows activated word representations to send activity back toward the phonemes that form those words. A partly matching sound activates a candidate word, and that word increases support for the phonemes compatible with its form.","children":[]},{"text":"The interaction is reciprocal because phonetic evidence affects lexical activation, and lexical activation changes the balance among phoneme interpretations. Forward cascading and backward feedback are separate properties, although both can operate while a word is being recognized.","children":[]}],"speech2-18":[{"text":"**Ganong (1980)** tested **how phoneme identification is impacted by word knowledge**, using ambiguous consonants in continua with a real word at one endpoint and a nonword at the other. **Ganong (1980)** used word/nonword pairs such as **corpse/gorpse** and varied the initial consonant across its category boundary in **5-millisecond steps**.","children":[]},{"text":"One endpoint produces a real word while the other produces a nonword. A listener’s choice can therefore be influenced both by the acoustic evidence for the consonant and by whether that interpretation forms a familiar lexical item.","children":[]}],"speech2-19":[{"text":"A **late correction process** leaves categorical perception functioning as usual but introduces a **bias to report having heard a word** after the initial phoneme decision. For corpse/gorpse, the response process favors the interpretation that produces corpse.","children":[]},{"text":"Under this account, word knowledge changes the report after phoneme identification. A general word-reporting bias is predicted across the continuum, including positions away from the most ambiguous category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"The **word–nonword**, **neutral**, and **nonword–word** conditions differ in which phoneme response creates a real word. The late-correction prediction shifts those responses without requiring lexical knowledge to change the earlier phoneme categorization itself.","children":[]}],"speech2-20":[{"text":"If **word knowledge** works to **shift the categorical boundary**, the location where listeners switch between phoneme labels changes toward an interpretation that forms a real word. The same ambiguous sound can receive different phoneme interpretations depending on which interpretation produces a real word.","children":[]},{"text":"The lexical effect is strongest **closest to the category boundary**. At the endpoints, the acoustic evidence strongly supports one consonant, whereas near the boundary lexical information can resolve uncertainty about the initial consonant.","children":[]},{"text":"The predicted difference between lexical conditions therefore rises near the ambiguous region and falls toward the endpoints. It differs from a general reporting bias that remains similar across continuum positions.","children":[]}],"speech2-21":[{"text":"Ganong’s results are consistent with **shifts** in phoneme labeling, with the largest word-related difference near the category boundary rather than an equal correction across the whole continuum. The labeling functions shift according to which consonant produces a real word, with smaller differences toward the clearer endpoints.","children":[]},{"text":"That pattern is consistent with a change in the phoneme boundary rather than a uniform word-reporting bias. Ganong considered guessing strategies, while interactive models implemented the effect through **feedback from lexical representations to phoneme representations**.","children":[]}],"speech2-22":[{"text":"In a **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model**, the initial input is ambiguous between **/b/** and **/p/** before **/l/**, the vowel in plug, and the final **/g/** supply more information over time. Both possible onsets initially receive activation because the first acoustic information supports more than one interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"As the following segments arrive, compatible words become active. Early /b/ support can contribute to candidates beginning with bl-, while /p/ support contributes to candidates beginning with pl-. Activation changes as each candidate is matched against more of the input.","children":[]},{"text":"Before the ending is complete, **plug, blood, and blush** can all receive support from partial matches. The final /g/ strongly favors plug over the competing interpretation of the initial consonant. Activation for the compatible word increases while the alternatives lose support, and feedback increases the phoneme activity consistent with that word.","children":[]}],"speech2-23":[{"text":"As **input unfolds over time**, activity accumulates for **plug**, especially when the **final /g/** arrives and conflicts with **blood** and **blush**, which can also be partly active. As the input unfolds, plug accumulates more activation than the candidates that were temporarily compatible with the beginning.","children":[]},{"text":"Increased activation for plug feeds back to the phoneme level and strengthens **/p/**, the onset consistent with the whole word. Later information can therefore change the balance between interpretations of an earlier ambiguous sound.","children":[]},{"text":"This **top-down processing** within TRACE allows word-level activity to influence phoneme-level activity. The lexical context contributes to the category boundary through the model’s connections rather than being applied only as a final reporting correction.","children":[]}],"speech2-24":[{"text":"The **TRACE model is a version of the Interactive Activation model for written word recognition**, adapted to spoken input whose acoustic information unfolds over time. Compatible representations at different levels support one another, while competing alternatives limit one another’s activity.","children":[]},{"text":"In written-word recognition, visual features support letters and letters support words such as **WORK, WORD, WEAR, and WORM**. Word activation can feed back to compatible letters, allowing incomplete visual input to be interpreted using lexical knowledge.","children":[]},{"text":"In speech, evidence arrives across time rather than as a completed written string. TRACE therefore represents features, phonemes, and words at possible temporal positions. A candidate can receive early support and then lose it when later sounds no longer fit.","children":[]}],"speech2-25":[{"text":"**Given the same physical difference, it is more difficult to distinguish stimuli from the same category than from different categories**, so sensitivity depends partly on the category relationship between sounds. **Categorical perception** describes this relationship between category membership and discrimination.","children":[]},{"text":"Acoustic ambiguity still affects **word recognition**, and word-level information affects **phoneme categorization**. A categorical final label therefore coexists with continuing interaction between the levels.","children":[]},{"text":"Spoken-word recognition begins while phonetic uncertainty is still being resolved. Partial input can activate lexical competitors, and lexical feedback can then influence the phoneme interpretation.","children":[]}],"speech2-26":[],"speech2-27":[{"text":"In **Allopenna and colleagues’ spoken-word recognition task**, participants heard words while viewing objects, allowing their changing eye movements to be measured as the spoken word unfolded. For a target such as **beaker**, a **cohort competitor** such as **beetle** shared the beginning, a **rhyme competitor** such as **speaker** shared the ending, and an unrelated object such as **carriage** shared neither.","children":[]},{"text":"Target and cohort looks increased early because the initial sounds were compatible with both words. As additional sounds distinguished beaker from beetle, target looking continued to rise while cohort looking declined.","children":[]},{"text":"Rhyme competition followed a different time course because the overlap occurred later in the word. Eye movements therefore reflected the order in which matching and mismatching sound information became available, before the final response was completed.","children":[]}],"speech2-28":[{"text":"Eye movements give an idea about **changes in activity for different competitors over time**, while the **TRACE simulation** predicts changing activation for the target and competing words before the final response. Cohort competition rises early and falls after the input differentiates the words, while rhyme competition has a later and smaller profile.","children":[]},{"text":"A final correct response conceals that temporary competition. Fixation probability measured over milliseconds preserves when alternatives gained support and when they lost it, allowing the time course of recognition to be compared with the model’s changing activity.","children":[]}],"sentence-01":[],"sentence-02":[{"text":"In **“The horse raced past the barn fell,”** the words **the horse raced past the barn** initially support an interpretation in which raced is the main verb describing the horse’s action. That interpretation becomes difficult when **fell** supplies another finite verb with no new subject.","children":[]},{"text":"In the grammatical reduced-relative interpretation, **raced past the barn** identifies which horse is meant, and **fell** states what happened to that horse. The same early words support more than one structure until the continuation makes one analysis untenable.","children":[]}],"sentence-03":[{"text":"The **garden path effect** occurs when an initially plausible structure for **“The horse raced past the barn fell”** conflicts with the word fell and requires a different grammatical analysis. In **“The horse raced past the barn fell,”** the reader can commit to raced as the main verb and then have difficulty accommodating fell.","children":[]},{"text":"The sentence permits the meaning of a horse that was raced past the barn and then fell. Its grammaticality therefore differs from how readily a listener or reader arrives at that interpretation. Processing difficulty can arise from an initially preferred analysis rather than from an impossible sentence.","children":[]}],"sentence-04":[{"text":"A **parser** constructs a **syntactic tree for the sentences we hear or read**, identifying which words belong together and how phrases relate within the sentence. It operates with **limited memory** and **time constraints**, so representing every possible interpretation in full would place demands on the cognitive system.","children":[]},{"text":"The reduced-relative analysis contains an embedded clause inside the noun phrase. The horse is modified by the equivalent of **that was raced past the barn**, and **fell** is the main verb. The simpler initial analysis instead treats **raced past the barn** as the main verb phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"A **noun phrase (NP)** groups a noun with its associated material, a **verb phrase (VP)** groups the verb with its complements, and a **prepositional phrase (PP)** includes a preposition and its object. Assigning these relationships determines how the words contribute to the sentence rather than merely identifying their individual meanings.","children":[]}],"sentence-05":[{"text":"**Minimal attachment** is a strategy to **initially prefer smaller trees**, choosing the syntactic analysis with fewer nodes rather than building every possible structure at once. A node represents a constituent or grammatical grouping, so a smaller tree uses fewer such groupings.","children":[]},{"text":"Treating **raced** as the main verb yields a relatively simple analysis. Treating **raced past the barn** as a reduced relative requires an embedded clause within the noun phrase, while **fell** remains outside it as the main predicate.","children":[]},{"text":"The smaller-tree preference reduces the initial structural commitment in memory. It can also produce a garden path when the simpler analysis is incompatible with the eventual continuation.","children":[]}],"sentence-06":[{"text":"**Late closure** is a **bias to keep the current clause open as long as possible**, attaching incoming material to the clause or phrase already being processed. In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,”** **with the dynamite** is preferentially associated with **blew open**.","children":[]},{"text":"Under that attachment, the dynamite is the instrument used in opening the safe. An alternative attachment places the prepositional phrase inside **the safe with the dynamite**, making it describe the safe instead.","children":[]},{"text":"The words support both structural possibilities, but the parser is biased toward the verb-phrase attachment. The structural decision determines whether the phrase specifies an instrument of the action or a property of the object.","children":[]}],"sentence-07":[{"text":"In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels,”** the initial verb-phrase attachment momentarily treats **the jewels as the material used to blow open the safe**, although that interpretation is implausible. The sensible interpretation instead groups **with the jewels** with **the safe**, identifying the safe containing them.","children":[]},{"text":"Reading times and eye movements indicate slower processing when the sensible interpretation requires the **unpreferred attachment**. The parser initially adopts one relationship and then revises it when the resulting event interpretation is unsuitable.","children":[]}],"sentence-08":[{"text":"An autonomous account proposes a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure of the sentence**, applying its own operating preferences rather than using meaning to choose the initial structure. **Minimal attachment** favors smaller trees, while **late closure** favors attaching new material to the current structure.","children":[]},{"text":"The account initially handles ambiguity by committing to a preferred parse instead of fully activating every possible structure. Revision becomes necessary when the continuation cannot be incorporated into that analysis.","children":[]},{"text":"In this proposal, meaning does not determine the earliest structural decision. Difficulty arises when a structurally preferred interpretation must later be replaced by an alternative compatible with the sentence.","children":[]}],"sentence-09":[{"text":"**Syntactically difficult sentences are not always difficult to parse**, including sentences with a **reduced relative clause** that modifies a noun without an explicit relative pronoun and auxiliary. In **“Recent research REPORTED BY FERREIRA AND CLIFTON…”**, reported identifies research that was reported, rather than describing research as the agent doing the reporting.","children":[]},{"text":"Other naturally occurring examples include **“Referential information PROVIDED BY THE DISCOURSE,” “results OBTAINED WITH GLOBAL READING TIMES,”** and **“examples CITED HERE.”** These forms are interpretable despite having the type of structure associated with garden paths.","children":[]},{"text":"**MacDonald, Perlmutter, and Seidenberg (1994)** propose that **semantic factors** and **statistical regularities** interact with parsing early. Structural form alone therefore does not determine how difficult every reduced relative will be.","children":[]}],"sentence-10":[{"text":"Reduced relatives occur within scientific prose in forms such as **“a noun phrase FOLLOWED BY A MORPHOLOGICALLY AMBIGUOUS VERB,” “the initial syntactic analysis ASSIGNED TO A SENTENCE,”** and **“differences LOCATED ON THE CRITICAL DISAMBIGUATING PHRASE,”** where the capitalized verbs introduce modifiers rather than new main clauses. The capitalized material **modifies the preceding noun**, so a reader has to treat it as part of that noun phrase rather than automatically interpreting its verb as the sentence’s main verb.","children":[]},{"text":"**“A person PRESENTED WITH THE EVIDENCE EXAMINED BY THE LAWYER…”** contains more than one such dependency, because presented modifies person while examined modifies evidence. Different words and surrounding meanings can make these structures interpretable rather than uniformly difficult.","children":[]},{"text":"The cited research examples were drawn partly from papers arguing for an autonomous parser. Their reduced relatives occur in the authors’ own readable prose, so the same broad construction can be difficult or readily understood depending on its lexical and semantic context.","children":[]}],"sentence-11":[{"text":"In **“Recent research reported by…”**, **research is an inanimate object and unlikely to be the subject of a verb like report**, rather than an entity that could normally perform the act of reporting. That meaning supports an interpretation in which someone else reported the research.","children":[]},{"text":"**Reported** occurs frequently in reduced relatives, especially in scientific writing after words such as research. Learned lexical statistics and knowledge of that writing context support the same grammatical analysis.","children":[]},{"text":"In **“The burglar arrested in the parking lot was charged today,”** the burglar is a plausible recipient of arrest. In **“The policeman arrested in the parking lot was charged today,”** the policeman is also a plausible agent of arresting, creating more competition with the required recipient interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"The structure is the same, but the nouns’ likely event roles differ. Meaning, word-specific construction frequencies, and broader world knowledge can therefore affect which parse is supported early.","children":[]}],"sentence-12":[{"text":"**World knowledge can be very local**, as in **Spivey and colleagues’ task (1995)**, where a visual world contained **a pencil, a box, a towel and an apple on a towel** while participants followed a spoken instruction. Participants heard **“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** and prepared the corresponding movement.","children":[]},{"text":"**On the towel** can temporarily be interpreted as the destination of put, making the empty towel a possible target. In the intended interpretation it identifies the apple’s current location, while **in the box** specifies the destination.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants often looked at the empty towel before shifting to the box. The **unambiguous** instruction **“Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”** explicitly marks the location phrase as a modifier and produces less temporary consideration of the towel as a destination.","children":[]}],"sentence-13":[{"text":"**World knowledge can be very local** in a two-referent context, where one apple is on a towel and another is on a different support, creating two possible objects for the same noun. The phrase **the apple on the towel** specifies which apple should be moved.","children":[]},{"text":"Eye movements pass from the starting fixation to the target apple and then the box, rather than making the same detour toward an incorrect towel destination. The sequence and timing are similar for the ambiguous instruction and the explicitly relative-clause version.","children":[]},{"text":"A modifier identifying the apple is useful when more than one apple is available. The local referential context therefore favors interpreting **on the towel** as part of the noun phrase.","children":[]}],"sentence-14":[{"text":"With **one apple in the display**, the instruction makes the empty towel a tempting but incorrect destination, while having **two apples in the display** changes how the same ambiguous words can identify the intended object. With **two apples**, participants make substantially fewer such looks.","children":[]},{"text":"The explicitly unambiguous instruction produces relatively few incorrect-destination looks in either context. The major ambiguity cost is therefore concentrated in the one-referent condition.","children":[]},{"text":"Two possible apples make additional identifying information appropriate. The phrase **on the towel** can select the intended apple, reducing the temporary interpretation of towel as the movement goal.","children":[]}],"sentence-15":[{"text":"**Different parses of the same sentence seem to be favored under different semantic or pragmatic conditions**, with the available meaning and communicative context affecting the interpretation very early. An apple’s location can function as an identifying property when another apple is present, but can temporarily be taken as a movement destination when only one apple is available.","children":[]},{"text":"Local visual context changes incorrect-destination looks during sentence processing. The structural decision is therefore sensitive to contextual information before the final movement is planned, rather than being determined by syntax alone and corrected only afterward.","children":[]}],"sentence-16":[{"text":"The **actual execution of arm movements** can be influenced by **syntactic ambiguity and its interaction with the visually-defined context**, so uncertainty can affect the mouse trajectory as well as eye movements. In a one-referent context, ambiguous and unambiguous instructions produce more separated movement paths than in a two-referent context.","children":[]},{"text":"The ambiguity affects the actual developing arm movement, not only where the eyes look. Greater trajectory separation during the movement indicates that the competing interpretation is influencing action while it is being resolved.","children":[]},{"text":"A two-referent context reduces that interference because the identifying modifier is more compatible with the available objects. Sentence interpretation and movement planning can therefore interact continuously.","children":[]}],"sentence-17":[],"sentence-18":[{"text":"**The** is a determiner that begins the noun phrase. It introduces a noun phrase and supports the expectation that a noun or material modifying a noun will follow.","children":[]}],"sentence-19":[{"text":"**Pizza** is a noun naming the referent being discussed. Its lexical category allows it to serve as the head of a noun phrase, while its meaning identifies a kind of food.","children":[]}],"sentence-20":[{"text":"**Was** is a finite form of be that supplies the sentence with a past-tense auxiliary. It contributes past tense and supports more than one possible continuation, including a description of a location or a passive construction.","children":[]}],"sentence-21":[{"text":"**In** is a preposition expressing a relationship such as location. It begins a prepositional phrase and creates an expectation for a noun phrase naming the relevant place or container.","children":[]}],"sentence-22":[{"text":"**The** is a determiner rather than a noun. In a phrase beginning with in, it supports an expectation for a noun or noun-modifying material within the preposition’s complement.","children":[]}],"sentence-23":[{"text":"**Eaten** is the participial form of eat rather than its simple past-tense form. In the sequence **“in the eaten,”** it occupies a position where the intended construction calls for a noun, producing a **word-category violation** rather than merely an unlikely food-related meaning.","children":[]}],"sentence-24":[{"text":"The **early left anterior negativity**, or **ELAN**, is an **ERP phenomenon elicited for word class violations**, an **event-related potential** associated with a word appearing in an unexpected grammatical category. An ERP is a brain response measured relative to the onset of an event such as a word. The ELAN occurs about **100–200 milliseconds** after the critical item and is strongest at left/anterior recording locations.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The pizza was in the eaten”** supplies a participial form where a noun is expected. **“The scientist criticized Max’s of proof the theorem”** puts the preposition of in a position incompatible with the expected noun phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"The mismatch concerns **grammatical category**, because a noun, verb, or preposition appears where the sentence predicts another type. The early timing prompted proposals that the response reflects rapid syntactic analysis.","children":[]}],"sentence-25":[{"text":"With **visually presented words**, the early category-sensitive response came from **visual cortex**, connecting its location to the system that processes the physical forms of written words. In **Dikker and colleagues’ account (2010)**, an expectation for a grammatical category feeds back to visual processing and predicts forms associated with that category.","children":[]},{"text":"For **“The beautiful princess…”** versus **“The beautifully princess…”**, the visible ending changes how the word fits the expected continuation. A physically available morphological cue can contribute to the mismatch before the whole sentence is interpreted.","children":[]},{"text":"**Form typicality** is how characteristic a word’s form is of a category. An early response to an unexpected category can therefore partly reflect a mismatch between an expected physical word pattern and the form actually received.","children":[]}],"sentence-26":[{"text":"In **“Chris and Ben are glad that the bird perches seem easy to install,”** the form **perches** names the supports birds sit on, so it functions as a noun despite also having a possible verb interpretation. In **“…the bird perches comfortably in the cage,”** the same form is a verb describing what the bird does.","children":[]},{"text":"In **“The teacher told the principal that the student needs were not being met,”** **needs** is a noun referring to requirements. In **“…the student needs to be more focused,”** needs is a verb. The continuation resolves the category of each ambiguous form.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological form typicality** describes how similar a word’s form is to the forms of nouns or verbs. The corpus analysis of **3,158 words** compares each form’s average distance from noun and verb forms. Noun-like verbs and verb-like nouns separate a word’s grammatical role from the category its form resembles.","children":[]}],"sentence-27":[{"text":"**Perches is noun-like and needs is verb-like** in their form-based tendencies, meaning that their written forms provide different cues about which grammatical category is likely. These are statistical properties of their forms rather than restrictions on their grammatical roles. Both words can function as nouns or as verbs.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The bird perches seem easy to install”** aligns a noun-like form with a noun use, whereas **“the bird perches comfortably in the cage”** requires a verb use of that noun-like form. **“The student needs were not being met”** requires a noun use of a verb-like form, while **“the student needs to be more focused”** aligns form typicality with a verb use.","children":[]},{"text":"A typical noun is closer to the noun-form distribution, and a typical verb is closer to the verb-form distribution. Forms that resemble the other category allow the effects of those learned similarities to be tested independently of the category required by the sentence.","children":[]}],"sentence-28":[{"text":"For a **noun-like ambiguous form such as perches**, resolving the form as a verb in **“the bird perches comfortably in the cage”** slows reading more than resolving it as a noun. When a **verb-like ambiguous word** is resolved as a noun, the slowdown is larger than when it is resolved as a verb.","children":[]},{"text":"The measure is the length-adjusted difference between reading time at **disambiguation** and at the earlier **point of ambiguity**. Positive values indicate increased time as the sentence makes the required category clear.","children":[]},{"text":"Perches and needs can keep the visible critical form constant while its role changes with the continuation. The reading-time cost is larger when the required category conflicts with the form’s typicality, linking category assignment with learned form statistics.","children":[]}],"sentence-29":[{"text":"The **ELAN effect for nouns where a verb is expected** depends on whether there is **syntactic or statistical form-based information** physically available to support the mismatch. It occurs for nouns with **morphological marking** and for nouns with strong **statistical noun-like form**, but not for neutral nouns such as **infant**.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparisons include **“The beautiful princess”** versus **“The beautifully princess,” “The tasteless soda”** versus **“The tastelessly soda,”** and **“The cute infant”** versus **“The cutely infant.”** Princess has an informative morphological form, soda is statistically noun-like, and infant supplies weaker category-form evidence.","children":[]},{"text":"An abstract category violation alone therefore does not determine the earliest visual response. The physical word form and the visual expectations associated with grammatical context contribute to that response.","children":[]}],"sentence-30":[{"text":"**“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”** expresses the partial independence of **syntax and semantics**, because its grammatical organization is acceptable even though its combined meaning is anomalous. Syntax and semantics are partly independent because a well-formed structure can combine words into a sentence without a plausible event interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The horse raced past the barn fell”** creates difficulty when the preferred early parse cannot accommodate the final verb. Differences between preferred and unpreferred reading times are consistent with parsing shortcuts that reduce memory demands instead of maintaining every possible structure.","children":[]},{"text":"**“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** receives different early interpretations in different referential contexts. Context and pragmatics can affect syntactic processing before the sentence is complete.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The bird perches were comfortable”** uses a noun-like form as a noun. Early category-related brain effects depend partly on form typicality and predictions about physical word forms, rather than being a pure measure of an abstract morphosyntactic structure.","children":[]}],"sentence-31":[],"aphasias-01":[],"aphasias-02":[{"text":"**Aphasia** involves losses of language function following brain damage, and historical models attributed **specific losses of function to disconnections between centers** responsible for different operations. In the 1870s, **Lichtheim and Wernicke** explained particular losses as disconnections between functional centers rather than treating every patient as having the same loss of language.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Lichtheim’s House model**, **A** stores sound images, **M** stores movement images for speech, and **B** represents concepts. The acoustic pathway **a** carries incoming sound to A, while the motor pathway **m** carries output from M toward articulation.","children":[]},{"text":"Understanding a spoken word requires the recognized sound pattern in A to reach its meaning in B. Verbatim repetition requires the sound representation in A to reach the speech-movement representation in M. Damage to different connections can therefore produce different failures even when some individual centers remain functional.","children":[]},{"text":"The numbered damage locations distinguish a damaged center from a disconnected pathway. **1** damages M, **2** damages A, **3** interrupts A–M, **4** interrupts M–B, **6** interrupts A–B, **5** interrupts motor output, and **7** interrupts acoustic input. The model assigns different tasks to different routes through the same system.","children":[]}],"aphasias-03":[{"text":"The **Geschwind-Wernicke model of brain and language**, revived by **Norman Geschwind in the 1970s**, relates language functions to brain regions and the connections through which those regions communicate. **Broca’s area** is anterior, in the frontal region, while **Wernicke’s area** is farther posterior in the temporal region. The **arcuate fasciculus** connects these regions through a pathway of nerve fibers.","children":[]},{"text":"The **central sulcus**, also called the fissure of Rolando, and the **lateral sulcus**, also called the fissure of Sylvius, are major anatomical grooves. The lateral sulcus lies above the temporal region and helps locate it relative to the frontal region.","children":[]},{"text":"Linguistic theory also distinguished **syntax**, the organization of words into grammatical structures, from other language processes. A grammar with its own computational principles could be impaired differently from word knowledge or meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"A **dissociation** is an impairment of one function alongside relative preservation of another. A **double dissociation** adds the reverse pattern in a different patient. Separating grammar from lexical processing produces more specific hypotheses about the language functions affected by damage.","children":[]}],"aphasias-04":[{"text":"In **Fromkin’s description (1997)**, **Broca’s aphasia** includes pauses while trying to find words, loss of **grammatical morphemes**, and frequently disturbed word order, affecting the normal grammatical connections between content words. **Grammatical morphemes** include small words and word parts that express grammatical relationships. Losing them can leave content words without the normal connections of a sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"Everyday auditory comprehension can seem relatively good, while controlled testing reveals substantial impairment. **Agrammatism** is closely associated with Broca’s aphasia, but the relationship is imperfect because some patients with Broca-area lesions are not agrammatic, and some agrammatic patients do not meet the neurological classification of Broca’s aphasia.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** involves relatively fluent speech with good intonation and pronunciation, but frequent word substitutions, **neologisms**, phonological errors, and comprehension difficulties. A **neologism** is an invented word form produced in place of an established word. Substitutions can be related or unrelated in meaning to the intended word, so fluency does not guarantee a coherent message.","children":[]}],"aphasias-05":[{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** is described as **word salad or Jabberwocky, often with neologisms**, because fluent output can contain inappropriate word choices or invented forms that make the intended meaning difficult to recover. A patient reporting poor vision said, **“My wires don’t hire right.”** The subject–verb structure is recognizable, but the selected words do not communicate the intended visual complaint.","children":[]},{"text":"Another patient answered a question about health with **“I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds…”** Familiar phrase structures continue while the message loses coherence. An aphasic physician used **“demaploze”** and **“tubaboys,”** invented forms placed within grammatical-sounding phrases.","children":[]},{"text":"Object descriptions could also be fluent but inappropriate. A fork was **“a need for a schedule,”** and a spoon was **“How many schemes on your throat.”** Words are being produced, but their selection does not produce an adequate description of the object.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** can produce **telegraphic speech**, with relative preservation of important content words and difficulty with grammatical particles and word order. A patient’s hospital account included **“Monday,” “Dad,” “hospital,” “two doctors,” “thirty minutes,” “Wednesday,” “nine o’clock,” “Thursday,” “ten o’clock,”** and **“teeth.”** Pauses and missing grammatical connections make the account halting, although the surviving content conveys more of the intended event than the fluent Wernicke-type examples.","children":[]}],"aphasias-06":[{"text":"A traditional distinction describes **Wernicke’s aphasia as problems with activation or inhibition in the lexicon**, while **Broca’s aphasia involves problems with grammar**, separating difficulties selecting words from difficulties organizing grammatical relations. **Activation** makes a word available, while **inhibition** suppresses competitors that should not be selected.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s original patient Leborgne**, nicknamed **Tan**, could produce essentially one syllable but appeared to retain other abilities and to understand what was said. His inability to articulate speech was different from the agrammatic but partly interpretable output associated with the later syndrome.","children":[]},{"text":"The original cases may be better described in terms of **apraxia of speech**, a difficulty organizing speech movements. A motor-production impairment and an impairment of grammatical knowledge are different proposed causes of limited speech output.","children":[]}],"aphasias-07":[{"text":"A **sharp distinction between the grammar and the lexicon** separates the abstract rules that combine words into sentences from the stored word representations available for those combinations. In the formal linguistic account, understanding a new sentence involves the grammatical process that generates its structure, rather than matching it to an already memorized sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"**“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”** is grammatical despite its unusual meaning. **“Furiously sleep ideas green colorless”** contains the same words but lacks the normal grammatical organization. Familiarity with a meaningful event cannot by itself explain the difference.","children":[]},{"text":"The proposed aphasia division assigns disrupted lexical activation to Wernicke’s aphasia and disrupted grammatical structure to Broca’s aphasia. This is a hypothesis about separable processes, beyond the descriptive contrast between fluent and nonfluent speech.","children":[]}],"aphasias-08":[{"text":"In the speech-production model, **functional and positional levels** differ from the **message level**, where a speaker selects a story angle and organizes the intended concepts, referents, and event structure. At the **functional level**, the first linguistic level, word meanings and semantic roles are selected.","children":[]},{"text":"**Lemmas** connect intended concepts with lexical items, and the relationship between a word and its referent is generally arbitrary. At the **positional level**, word forms are placed in grammatically appropriate order and abstract processes organize syntactic structure and affixes.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological encoding** supplies the sound forms, followed by speech gestures for articulation. Difficulty can therefore arise at a conceptual, lexical, structural, sound-form, or motor stage rather than at one undifferentiated language operation.","children":[]},{"text":"Compounds such as **HOUSEBOAT** and **DOGHOUSE** have meanings related to their parts, but their construction still involves combining forms. Speech errors such as **“easy enoughly”** support morphological operations at the positional level rather than requiring every derived form to be selected as an indivisible lemma.","children":[]}],"aphasias-09":[{"text":"Under an autonomous parsing account, **the syntactic structure of sentences is computed independently of meaning as we listen or read**, giving structural interpretation its own processing mechanism. A patient could use word meanings and contextual cues to understand ordinary conversation while still having difficulty computing a sentence’s grammatical relationships.","children":[]},{"text":"In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,”** the phrase **with the dynamite** can attach to the verb phrase as an instrument or to the noun phrase as a modifier of the safe. The words remain the same while the structural relationship changes.","children":[]},{"text":"Testing a specific parsing deficit requires materials that separate those structural demands from easier routes based on familiar word meanings or likely events. General success in conversation does not establish equal success on all sentence structures.","children":[]}],"aphasias-10":[{"text":"Broca distinguished **articulate speech from comprehension**, separating the ability to produce an intelligible spoken response from the ability to understand language heard from someone else. A patient who understood speech but could not produce it supplied evidence that the two abilities could be affected differently.","children":[]},{"text":"A broader **syntactic impairment** would concern grammatical organization in both production and comprehension. Establishing that impairment requires testing the patient’s interpretation of structural relationships, rather than equating an inability to speak with an inability to represent grammar.","children":[]}],"aphasias-11":[{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia is almost always accompanied with some level of motor dysfunction**, so difficulties producing speech can interfere with the expression of grammatical knowledge even when other language abilities remain available. A person may omit grammatical material because speech is effortful, preserving the words that communicate the most information. Agrammatic output can therefore reflect how a person communicates despite impairment rather than direct loss of grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"**Competence** refers to linguistic knowledge, while **performance** is the use of that knowledge in a particular task. Motor difficulties can impair performance even when the person retains more knowledge than the utterance expresses.","children":[]},{"text":"**Comprehension testing** measures sentence interpretation without requiring a full spoken answer. In the account examined by **Caramazza and Zurif (1976)**, persistent structural difficulty in this task would extend beyond the physical effort of articulation.","children":[]}],"aphasias-12":[{"text":"In conversation, **there are lots of redundant cues to what a speaker intends**, so **“The dog bit the man”** can be understood through word meanings, event plausibility, ordinary word order, and discourse context. The words DOG, BITE, and MAN support a familiar event, and English canonical **subject–verb–object** order also supports interpreting the first noun as the agent. Conversation often adds a discourse context that further clarifies the intended event.","children":[]},{"text":"A **heuristic** is a simplifying strategy that substitutes a regular pattern for a fuller analysis of the sentence. A listener relying on likely events or the first-noun-as-agent pattern could reach the right interpretation without successfully analyzing every grammatical relationship.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The dog that the man is biting is angry”** assigns the biting action to the man. The dog is being bitten, although it is the first noun and dogs are more typical biters. Recovering the intended meaning requires separating the main-clause subject from the agent inside the relative clause.","children":[]}],"aphasias-13":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif devised a list of sentences and a set of control sentences** with straightforward actions, including **“The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird,” “The girl is pulling a red wagon,”** and **“The man is reading a yellow book.”** The remaining controls describe a boy riding a new bicycle, a girl holding a broken doll, a boy throwing a round ball, and a boy carrying a sharp pencil. The agent normally occurs first, and familiar event knowledge supports the interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"In **semantically constrained center-embedded sentences**, one clause occurs inside another but likely event roles strongly support who acts on what. The materials include **“The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green,” “The book that the girl is reading is yellow,”** and **“The bat that the boy is carrying is brown.”** Other items concern a boy holding a bicycle, a girl wearing a coat, a woman washing a dress, and a clown holding a balloon.","children":[]},{"text":"In **reversible center-embedded sentences**, either participant could plausibly perform the action. The materials include **“The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown,” “The cat that the dog is biting is black,”** and **“The fish that the frog is biting is green.”** Other pairs involve a tiger chasing a lion, a boy hitting a girl, a woman hugging a man, and a boy kicking a girl. Grammar specifies which participant is the agent because exchanging the roles still produces a possible event.","children":[]},{"text":"**Improbable center-embedded sentences** make ordinary event expectations favor the wrong direction. The items include **“The horse that the bird is kicking is brown,” “The lion that the baby is scaring is yellow,” “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,”** and **“The dog that the man is biting is black.”** Other items assign riding to a horse acting on a man, spanking to a boy acting on a woman, chasing to a robber acting on a policeman, and patting to a cat acting on a girl. The embedded-clause noun performs the action even when that relationship is unusual.","children":[]}],"aphasias-14":[{"text":"In **a sentence-picture matching task**, healthy controls are **more or less equally good at all stimulus types**, including control, semantically constrained, reversible, and improbable sentences that place different demands on interpreting roles. Their accuracy changes little when likely event roles stop supporting the grammatical interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s patients** perform poorly overall, consistent with broader comprehension difficulties. **Broca’s patients** have greater difficulty when they must keep track of who did what to whom without relying on familiar semantic roles.","children":[]},{"text":"The Broca group performs better when plausibility supports the grammatical relationship and worse for reversible or improbable materials. Caramazza and Zurif interpreted this selective comprehension difficulty as evidence for a syntactic impairment, beyond the patients’ difficulties producing speech.","children":[]}],"aphasias-15":[{"text":"The distinction between **the grammar and the lexicon** separates difficulties with structural relations among words from difficulties with word representations and their activation, making the proposed impaired operation more specific. Impairments of these processes predict different patterns across tasks, even when two patients have similar overall fluency.","children":[]},{"text":"A person can retain a content word’s meaning but struggle to assign it the correct role in a complex sentence. Another person can produce a fluent sentence frame while selecting inappropriate words. Structure–function hypotheses therefore concern the specific operation affected, rather than classifying every fluent or nonfluent patient as having the same deficit.","children":[]}],"aphasias-16":[{"text":"In **Dick and colleagues’ task**, participants listen to a sentence and **indicate which animal is doing the action**, identifying the agent rather than simply reporting which animal names they heard. The task holds the event constant while changing its grammatical form.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The dog is biting the cat”** is an **active** sentence, and **“It’s the dog that is biting the cat”** is a **subject cleft**. Both place the dog, the agent, before the cat.","children":[]},{"text":"**“It’s the cat that the dog is biting”** is an **object cleft**, and **“The cat is bitten by the dog”** is a **passive**. Both begin with the cat, the participant receiving the action. Correctly identifying the actor requires the grammatical relationship rather than simply choosing the first noun.","children":[]}],"aphasias-17":[{"text":"When **aphasia patients and healthy aging controls** interpret active, subject-cleft, object-cleft, and passive sentences, healthy controls perform accurately across the forms while patient groups differ in their comprehension patterns. Patients with **anomic aphasia** perform better overall than several other aphasia groups but still lose accuracy for the harder forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** and **conduction aphasia** are associated with a marked decline for object clefts and lower performance for passives than the easiest forms. **Wernicke’s patients** have poor accuracy across all four forms.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca’s patients have particular difficulty with noncanonical structures, but their active-sentence performance is also below that of healthy controls. The impairment combines a general reduction in performance with additional vulnerability when the grammatical arrangement is harder.","children":[]}],"aphasias-18":[{"text":"**Healthy undergraduates under conditions that stress the language system** can also lose comprehension accuracy, even though they have no aphasia, when speech or competing task demands make processing more difficult. The manipulations include compressed or faster speech, low-pass filtering, noise, and remembering auditory or visual digit lists during the sentence task.","children":[]},{"text":"**Object-cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all manipulations**, while active sentences and subject clefts remain relatively easier. **Combined stress conditions**, including low-pass filtering with compression, produce larger losses than the normal listening condition.","children":[]},{"text":"These listeners have no aphasic brain injury, yet the harder structures produce a disproportionate deficit when processing is taxed. The corresponding pattern in aphasia can therefore arise partly from a **performance limitation**, rather than uniquely identifying loss of a grammatical rule.","children":[]}],"aphasias-19":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked patients to **match the action to the object**, identifying which object fits the meaning of an action presented either linguistically or without spoken language. In a nonverbal condition, a pictured action such as licking must be related to an object such as an ice cream rather than an unrelated choice. A linguistic condition conveys the relevant information with language.","children":[]},{"text":"Aphasic participants were less accurate in both conditions. Accuracy was about **93.4%** for nonlinguistic items and **87.5%** for linguistic items, compared with **98.5%** and **99.4%** for controls. Broca-type comprehension difficulties therefore extended to interpreting actions without words.","children":[]},{"text":"Semantically related distractors were more disruptive than unrelated choices. An incorrect object that is related in meaning competes more strongly with the intended action–object match.","children":[]}],"aphasias-20":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of the task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area**, linking poor action matching with injury in that region even when spoken sentence processing was absent. The affected behavior concerned interpreting pantomimed action rather than producing a spoken sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"The association links frontal damage with a comprehension demand outside language. A deficit tied to that region can therefore involve action interpretation as well as the grammatical difficulties measured with verbal materials.","children":[]}],"aphasias-21":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues asked patients to match environmental sounds to pictures**, so hearing an **environmental sound**, such as a cow mooing, required selecting the corresponding object rather than interpreting a spoken word. A verbal condition provides a spoken description such as **“cow mooing”** instead of the environmental sound.","children":[]},{"text":"The choices can include a semantically related object, such as another animal, or an unrelated object, such as a violin. Correct performance requires relating the auditory input to the event or object it represents.","children":[]},{"text":"Verbal and nonverbal accuracy was approximately **99.2% and 98.1%** for controls, **99.1% and 98.2%** after right-hemisphere damage, **98.6% and 96.7%** for anomic aphasia, **96.3% and 95.2%** for Broca’s aphasia, and **90.0% and 91.3%** for Wernicke’s aphasia.","children":[]},{"text":"Wernicke’s patients performed worst overall, and the verbal and nonverbal versions did not differ significantly. The difficulty therefore extended to retrieving meaning from environmental sounds rather than being restricted to spoken words.","children":[]}],"aphasias-22":[{"text":"**Deficits in both verbal and non-verbal sound-picture matching are associated with Wernicke’s area**, relating damage in that region to problems connecting auditory input with meaning across both kinds of stimulus. The lesion overlap for patients impaired on the nonverbal task and for patients impaired on the verbal task involved posterior language-related regions.","children":[]},{"text":"The same broad damage distribution was related to failures with words and with meaningful environmental sounds. Auditory comprehension deficits in these patients therefore extend beyond recognition of linguistic forms alone.","children":[]}],"aphasias-23":[{"text":"**How language-specific the deficits in aphasia are remains a controversy**, because particular language impairments coexist with evidence that some of the same patients struggle with nonlinguistic tasks. Some difficulties are more pronounced for particular grammatical constructions, while other deficits extend to nonverbal action or environmental-sound comprehension.","children":[]},{"text":"Behavioral tests quantify accuracy for controlled conditions, and lesion analyses relate those measurements to the location of brain damage. A structure–function explanation must account for both the specific task demands and the observed association between damaged tissue and performance.","children":[]}],"aphasias-24":[],"aphasias-25":[{"text":"**Formalizing structure-function relationships in aphasia data** uses **MRI data** and **voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping** to relate the location of damaged brain tissue to behavioral impairment across patients. A **voxel** is a small volume element in a brain image, allowing damaged and spared locations to be compared with measured symptoms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Fluency deficits** are associated more strongly with anterior regions, while **comprehension deficits** are associated more strongly with posterior regions. These are broad symptom measures rather than tests of one particular grammatical rule or lexical operation.","children":[]},{"text":"The pattern is consistent with a rough **double dissociation** between production and perception-related functions. Anterior and posterior damage have different behavioral associations across the patient sample.","children":[]}],"development1-01":[],"development1-02":[{"text":"The **nature and nurture** question in child language concerns how experience and innate capacities contribute to acquisition, and **nativist positions** develop from **Chomsky’s critique of Skinner**. Skinner’s behaviorist approach used general learning mechanisms such as reward and punishment. Chomsky argued that these mechanisms could not account for the grammar children acquire.","children":[]},{"text":"For Chomsky, **the important feature of language is its generativity**. Speakers can produce and understand new sentences rather than only repeat sentences already heard. **Acquiring a language means identifying the abstract grammar that produces sentences in the language**, the system of rules that makes new combinations possible.","children":[]},{"text":"Grammatical and ungrammatical sentences are not **“similar” in any straightforward way**. A small change can produce an ungrammatical sentence, while two grammatical sentences can look very different. Surface resemblance therefore does not directly separate the sentences a grammar permits from those it excludes.","children":[]},{"text":"The context of acquisition also does not resemble **operant conditioning**. Operant conditioning changes behavior through consequences such as reward or punishment, but children are not systematically rewarded for grammatical sentences and punished for ungrammatical ones. The required **reward/punishment contingencies** do not hold consistently.","children":[]},{"text":"Nativist approaches instead propose **Universal Grammar**, innate knowledge of what language is like, and **language-specific acquisition processes**. Built-in constraints would restrict the possible grammars a child considers, while specialized learning processes would identify the grammar of the surrounding language.","children":[]},{"text":"Chomsky connected this proposal to the rapid acquisition of comparably complex grammars by ordinary children. He proposed a specially equipped **“data-handling” or “hypothesis-formulating” ability**, a capacity to form candidate accounts of language from the available experience.","children":[]}],"development1-03":[{"text":"Early accounts treated **categorical perception as a language-specific and species-specific phenomenon**, in which similarity between sounds is defined partly in linguistic rather than purely physical terms. Synthetic syllables change in equal steps along a formant-transition continuum, but identification shifts abruptly among **/b/, /d/, and /g/**.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination is stronger near the category boundaries than within one category. The same number of acoustic steps is therefore easier to hear when it crosses a phoneme boundary. One-step, two-step, and three-step comparisons vary physical separation while preserving this relationship to category identity.","children":[]},{"text":"Early accounts treated categorical perception as **language-specific** and **species-specific**, because it was then known primarily for human perception of speech. Under that interpretation, the category organization belonged to a specialized language system rather than to hearing more generally.","children":[]}],"development1-04":[{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, an infant’s response to a detected sound change becomes measurable through an orienting head movement, rather than through a spoken judgment. The infant learns that an auditory event predicts a rewarding visual event and turns toward it.","children":[]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** uses sucking as the response measure. Repeated sound exposure can reduce responding, and a renewed response after a sound change indicates discrimination. Neither method requires the infant to pronounce the sound or report a verbal label.","children":[]}],"development1-05":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** was tested by **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** through controlled differences in **voice onset time**, the delay between releasing a stop consonant and beginning vocal-fold vibration. Sucking declined during repeated exposure before a sound change was introduced.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **20D** condition, the sound changed by **20 milliseconds** across different adult phoneme categories, from **+20 to +40 milliseconds**. Sucking increased again when the stimulus changed across the category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **20S** condition, the physical change was also 20 milliseconds, but the pair remained within one adult category, with values of **−20 to 0** or **+60 to +80 milliseconds**. The equally large change within a category produced much less recovery in sucking. In the **0** control, the sound stayed the same and responding continued to decline.","children":[]},{"text":"The boundary-crossing and within-category changes were physically matched. The stronger response to the boundary-crossing change indicates that the infant’s sensitivity was organized in relation to a phonetic category distinction rather than the size of the timing difference alone.","children":[]}],"development1-06":[{"text":"Categorical perception was considered a **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format that could be used by linguistic processes**, converting variable acoustic input into a representation suited to language. Its presence in very young infants was therefore taken as possible evidence for an innate speech-processing ability.","children":[]},{"text":"At about **four months**, infants are developing gross motor abilities such as manipulating objects with two hands and holding the head and chest up while lying prone. Color vision and general visual acuity are also still developing.","children":[]},{"text":"Vocal production is only beginning to include more vowel-like sounds rather than creaky whines. Category-related perception at this age precedes mature speech production, which contributed to the interpretation that the perceptual organization was available very early.","children":[]}],"development1-07":[{"text":"Through **perceptual narrowing for speech categories**, infants’ ability to discriminate some nonnative speech contrasts declines over development, even when those contrasts were distinguishable earlier in infancy. Around six months, English-learning infants can discriminate some contrasts they have not encountered in their own language.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **Werker and Tees** comparison, discrimination of tested **Hindi** and **Salish** contrasts is strong at **6–8 months**, lower at **8–10 months**, and much lower at **10–12 months**. Relevant Hindi or Salish experience is associated with stronger continuing performance.","children":[]},{"text":"Native-language development therefore changes which differences remain perceptually available. A phonetic distinction that is detectable early can become harder to perceive as the system becomes adapted to the language heard regularly.","children":[]}],"development1-08":[{"text":"Under the **citizens of the world** account, categorical perception is a special property of the language system that provides **innate, language-general knowledge about speech**, rather than depending entirely on learning particular contrasts. Early categorical-perception abilities are treated as a special property of the language system.","children":[]},{"text":"Under this account, learning **selects among innately specified phonetic contrasts**. Experience narrows a broad initial inventory to distinctions that matter in the child’s native language. The developmental change is primarily selection and tuning, rather than construction of all categories from an initially undifferentiated signal.","children":[]}],"development1-09":[{"text":"**Categorical perception is not limited to language** and is **not limited to humans**, because it occurs for nonspeech sounds and in nonhuman animals as well as during human speech perception. The perceptual pattern is also **task-dependent**, because the response demanded can affect how categorical behavior appears.","children":[]},{"text":"Infant perception is not equally sensitive to every possible speech contrast. Some distinctions are weak early and become stronger with relevant experience, so an account of development must include learning-related gains as well as narrowing.","children":[]}],"development1-10":[{"text":"**Categorical perception of non-speech sounds** occurs in **Cutting and Rosner’s study (1974)**, where changes in acoustic rise time affect how listeners identify and discriminate **sawtooth waves and sine waves**. Rise time is the interval over which a sound builds toward its stronger amplitude. A fast onset supports a plucked quality, while a slower onset supports a bowed quality.","children":[]},{"text":"Pluck labels decrease and bow labels increase across the sawtooth continuum. Discrimination peaks near the region where the labels exchange dominance, around the middle of the **0–80 millisecond** rise-time range.","children":[]},{"text":"Equally separated rise times are therefore not equally discriminable at every position. Category-related organization occurs for sounds that do not contain a speech contrast.","children":[]}],"development1-11":[{"text":"**Categorical perception of non-speech sounds by infants** was examined by **Jusczyk and colleagues (1977)**, who measured sucking responses after repeated exposure and a controlled change in the sound. The stimulus either stayed unchanged, changed within a perceptual category, or changed between categories.","children":[]},{"text":"Sucking remained relatively low after no change and after the within-category change. A between-category change produced a renewed response before sucking declined again.","children":[]},{"text":"The infants’ discrimination depended on the category relationship of the nonspeech sounds. Both the early developmental timing and the category-related response are therefore compatible with auditory organization beyond speech alone.","children":[]}],"development1-12":[{"text":"Chinchillas were **trained using operant conditioning** so that responding to a speech stimulus at one endpoint of a controlled continuum was associated with receiving food. Either the **0-millisecond-lag** or **80-millisecond-lag** stimulus acted as an **SD**, a signal that a response would receive its reward.","children":[]},{"text":"Testing intermediate values measured generalization along a **/ga/–/ka/** VOT continuum. The proportion of one response changed sharply across part of the continuum, broadly resembling the corresponding human identification pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"Increasing the frication burst and aspiration noise could change the boundary or its steepness. The categorization depended on acoustic properties and learned responses in an animal without human language, rather than requiring a human phoneme inventory.","children":[]}],"development1-13":[{"text":"**A place contrast for nasals in onset** is not equally easy for young infants to hear, as **Narayan and colleagues (2010)** found when comparing early sensitivity to **/ma/–/na/** and **/na/–/ŋa/**. **/ma/–/na/** changes between bilabial and alveolar nasals, while **/na/–/ŋa/** changes between alveolar and velar nasals.","children":[]},{"text":"At about **4–5 months**, English-hearing infants look longer on change trials for /ma/–/na/, indicating that they discriminate it. Looking changes much less between same and change trials for /na/–/ŋa/.","children":[]},{"text":"The /na/–/ŋa/ onset contrast is not readily perceived at this age even when language experience is considered, whereas /ma/–/na/ is relatively **salient**, or easy to detect. Broad infant sensitivity does not mean that every phonetic distinction begins equally clear.","children":[]}],"development1-14":[{"text":"Between **6 and 10 months**, **exposure to the contrast in a Filipino language environment increases its salience**, making the native **/na/–/ŋa/** difference easier for Filipino-learning infants to perceive. Younger infants at **6–8 months** have similar looking times for same and change trials, while infants at **10–12 months** look longer following the change.","children":[]},{"text":"The early English-hearing comparison has weak sensitivity to this contrast as well, despite better sensitivity to /ma/–/na/. The later improvement in the relevant language environment is therefore a gain in a previously weak distinction.","children":[]},{"text":"Some speech contrasts require environmental exposure for acquisition. Learning can strengthen category differentiation, rather than only remove distinctions from an already complete innate inventory.","children":[]}],"development1-15":[{"text":"**Emergentist approaches assume that associative learning mechanisms are sensitive to statistical regularities in the environment**, allowing language-related patterns to develop from the structure of the experienced input. An associative system changes according to which events repeatedly occur together or predict one another.","children":[]},{"text":"In conditioning, food activates a response such as salivation. Pairing a bell with food strengthens the connection through which the bell can come to activate that response. The association changes with the relation between the events.","children":[]},{"text":"Extending associative learning to **pattern recognition** means learning regularities among stimulus properties. Repeated acoustic relationships can shape how sounds are grouped without assigning every category in advance.","children":[]}],"development1-16":[{"text":"A **vowel space determined by the first and second formants**, **F1 and F2**, represents how vowels are distributed across different combinations of the vocal tract’s resonant frequencies. Repeated productions of a vowel form a distribution across these frequencies, and different vowel distributions can overlap.","children":[]},{"text":"In the statistical-learning account discussed by **Kuhl and others**, listeners extract regularities from those distributions. Frequently encountered combinations receive stronger support than rare combinations.","children":[]},{"text":"A **prototypical /a/** is proposed to reflect a central or especially frequent region of the experienced /a/ distribution. A **mean** is an average location, while a **mode** is the most frequent region. A learned prototype can therefore summarize the pattern of input rather than correspond to a preassigned acoustic value.","children":[]}],"development1-17":[{"text":"**Maye and colleagues had infants listen passively to two distributions of voice onset time**, differing in whether the input contained one concentration of examples or two separate concentrations. The **bimodal** distribution had two high-frequency regions and provided evidence for two categories. The **unimodal** distribution concentrated exposure near the middle and provided evidence for one category.","children":[]},{"text":"At test, infants heard **stimuli 3 and 6** on either **alternating** or **non-alternating trials**. Alternating trials changed between the two tokens, while non-alternating trials repeated one token. The test sounds were the same across exposure groups, so their previous distribution differed rather than the physical test contrast.","children":[]},{"text":"For **six-month-old unimodal** infants, mean looking was **4.85 seconds** for alternating and **4.53 seconds** for non-alternating trials. The corresponding **eight-month-old unimodal** values were **4.98 and 5.20 seconds**. These groups did not reliably differentiate the test conditions.","children":[]},{"text":"The **six-month-old bimodal** values were **5.66 and 6.41 seconds**, and the **eight-month-old bimodal** values were **5.45 and 6.15 seconds**. Only infants with bimodal exposure were sensitive to the difference between conditions.","children":[]},{"text":"Exposure to one central cluster led infants to treat stimuli 3 and 6 more like members of one category. Exposure to two clusters supported differentiating them, linking the distribution of heard sounds to perceptual organization.","children":[]}],"development1-18":[{"text":"The **same exposure to a Mandarin speaker is more effective for learning when it happens in person and is interactive than when it is viewed as a video**, even though both conditions provide spoken input. In the reported study, nine-month-old American infants received **twelve 25-minute sessions** and were then tested on a Mandarin contrast using a head-turn procedure.","children":[]},{"text":"The live Mandarin group performed better than the English-exposure control group. Television and audio-only Mandarin exposure did not produce the same learning benefit. The acoustic language exposure therefore had different consequences depending on the interactive context.","children":[]},{"text":"In a separate social-response manipulation, maternal feedback was either **contingent** on the infant’s vocalization or supplied at times unrelated to that infant’s current sound. Contingent social responses increased the proportion of syllable-like vocalizations more strongly than noncontingent responses.","children":[]},{"text":"Social interaction can act like a reinforcing event for vocal production. Proposed explanations include an innate attraction to social interaction and an early capacity to use other people’s social cues during learning.","children":[]}],"development1-19":[{"text":"**Statistical learning with constraints** means that learning depends partly on **what stimulus properties can be contrastive**, so not every variation in the acoustic signal becomes a new language category. Differences in speaker identity or speech rate vary across tokens, but they are not equivalent to differences defining a phoneme category.","children":[]},{"text":"Existing learning also changes how later input is organized. An established perceptual pattern can influence which new regularities are readily acquired.","children":[]},{"text":"The **social or interactive context for learning** constrains how acoustic input is used, including whether the sounds occur in an exchange with another person. Learning therefore depends on both the acoustic events and the conditions under which the learner attends to and interacts with them.","children":[]}],"development2-01":[],"development2-02":[{"text":"**There are no physical boundaries between words** consistently marking every division in the speech stream, yet listeners are able to find familiar word forms within this continuously varying **sea of sounds**. Brief silences can result from articulatory closures inside a word just as well as between words. A pause therefore does not consistently mark a lexical boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"Listeners nevertheless recover words from a changing acoustic stream. **Word segmentation** is the process of finding those units despite the absence of a reliable silence at each boundary.","children":[]}],"development2-03":[{"text":"**Words sometimes appear in isolation**, including **“Hello,” “No,” “Please,” “Go,”** and **“Stop,”** allowing their forms to be heard without a neighboring word even though many words are not encountered this way. Those tokens provide relatively clear word-sized units, but many words are heard mainly within connected speech.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonotactics** concerns the sound sequences permitted or common within a language. Sequences across **wordboundary** or **speechcode** can be unusual within one word, while **movement** cautions that apparent internal seams do not always divide separate words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Stress** provides another cue to how syllables are grouped into words. Many English words begin with a strong syllable. In **Jusczyk and colleagues’ study (1999)**, **7.5-month-olds** familiarized with **kingdom**, stressed on its first syllable, preferred passages containing that word. Infants familiarized with **device**, stressed on its second syllable, did not produce the same preference.","children":[]},{"text":"The kingdom passage places the word in phrases such as **“Your kingdom is in a faraway place”** and **“sail to that kingdom.”** The device passage similarly embeds device in connected sentences. The difference concerns finding a familiar word in a stream, where stress can favor a particular candidate boundary.","children":[]}],"development2-04":[{"text":"In **pretty baby**, **the syllable /ti/ is very strongly predicted by the syllable /prI/** within pretty, so hearing the first syllable supplies information about which syllable is likely to follow. The transition from the final syllable of pretty to the first syllable of baby crosses a word boundary and is less predictable.","children":[]},{"text":"A **transitional probability** measures how often a particular next syllable follows a given preceding syllable. It depends on the frequency of their combination relative to how often the first syllable occurs, rather than the combination’s count alone.","children":[]},{"text":"The frequency of pretty divided by the frequency of pre is much greater than the frequency of tyba divided by the frequency of ty. More reliable within-word transitions can therefore distinguish a recurring unit from a less predictable transition between units.","children":[]}],"development2-05":[{"text":"**The likelihood of syllable B following syllable A is greater within than between words**, making the predictability of the following syllable a probabilistic cue rather than an infallible physical boundary. A low-probability transition is evidence about a possible boundary rather than a physical separator.","children":[]},{"text":"**“What’s that in the road a/head?”** supports different groupings around a and head. **“I scream, you scream, we all scream for ice cream”** contains closely related sound sequences with different word boundaries. The acoustic sequence and its learned probabilities can support more than one segmentation.","children":[]}],"development2-06":[{"text":"**Saffran and colleagues (1996)** isolated **statistical information from other cues** by creating a continuously presented artificial language from three-syllable words such as **tokibu, gikoba, and gopila**. The stimuli were controlled so that regularities among syllables could be tested separately from other segmentation cues.","children":[]},{"text":"The stream was **monotone**, without systematic differences in pitch, duration, or amplitude marking the words. It used simple **consonant–vowel syllables**, preventing unusual phoneme combinations from identifying the boundaries.","children":[]},{"text":"The syllables were presented continuously, with **no pauses** between words. Recurring syllable groupings therefore had to be distinguished through the sequence statistics rather than stress, silence, or distinctive phonotactic transitions.","children":[]}],"development2-07":[{"text":"The continuous **tokibugikobagopila** stream contains recurring sequences such as **tokibu, gikoba, gopila, and tipolu**, but the heard input does not include pauses or spaces to announce their boundaries. A stretch such as **tokibugikobagopilatipolu** joins several intended units into one uninterrupted sequence.","children":[]},{"text":"The learner receives the syllables, not already marked word boundaries. Repeated transitions within the recurring units provide information about which syllables tend to belong together.","children":[]}],"development2-08":[{"text":"Within **tokibugikobagopila** and its different repetitions, **tokibu, gikoba, gopila, and tipolu** retain their internal syllable order even though one whole sequence can follow several different sequences. Each intended word is a stable sequence, while its neighboring word can vary.","children":[]},{"text":"**Within-word transitions** are therefore more consistent than transitions from a word’s last syllable to the next word’s first syllable. The heard stream remains continuous even when the statistical groupings are identified.","children":[]}],"development2-09":[{"text":"**Testing for an effect of transitional probabilities** requires distinguishing predictability from **frequency of occurrence**, because recognizing a repeated syllable sequence alone does not establish that its internal transitions were learned. Half the words occur more frequently than the other half, and test **part-words** cross intended boundaries while matching real words in occurrence count.","children":[]},{"text":"In **tokibu gikoba gopila gikoba tokibu gopila tokibu gikoba gikoba tokibu**, **GOPILA** and **BU-GIKO** each occur twice. Their equal counts do not make their internal transitions equally predictable.","children":["Both **GO–PI** and **PI–LA** have transitional probability **2/2**, because every relevant occurrence of the first syllable is followed by the same next syllable.","**BU–GI** has probability **2/4**, because BU occurs four times but only two occurrences lead into GI. A boundary-spanning part-word therefore contains a less reliable transition even though the full sequence occurs as often as GOPILA."]},{"text":"Distinguishing the word from that part-word requires information about how the syllables predict one another. Familiarity with a frequent three-syllable string alone cannot explain the contrast when its raw occurrence count is matched.","children":[]}],"development2-10":[{"text":"Infants **looked longer in the direction of part-word stimuli** than toward words from the artificial language, responding differently even though the tested words and part-words were matched in occurrence frequency. The part-word preference is a **novelty effect**, because the boundary-spanning sequence is treated differently from a learned word.","children":[]},{"text":"Because words and part-words were matched for occurrence frequency, the preference cannot be attributed only to hearing one test sequence fewer times. The infants were sensitive to statistical organization within the stream, including how reliably one syllable predicted another.","children":[]}],"development2-11":[{"text":"**Identifying word referents** means learning which entity or concept a recognized word names, so detecting the sound pattern of a word does not by itself determine its meaning. Recognizing the sound pattern **cat** and knowing which object it names are different learning problems.","children":[]},{"text":"Segmentation can identify a recurring word form without supplying its meaning. Learning the form–referent relationship requires selecting what in the surrounding situation the speaker intends that form to refer to.","children":[]}],"development2-12":[{"text":"The **inductive problem of early word learning** arises because the same encounter with a cat supports many possible meanings, including an animal category, an individual, an action, a property, or a part. A novel word near a cat could name the category **CAT**, the broader category **ANIMAL**, the role **PET**, or the individual **FELIX**.","children":[]},{"text":"It could instead describe **FURRY**, **ALIVE**, or **WALKING**, or refer to **WHISKERS** or a **LEG**. The same visible animal provides a whole object, properties, parts, actions, and broader or narrower classifications.","children":[]},{"text":"The **hypothesis space** is the set of candidate meanings compatible with that experience. Quine’s **gavagai problem** concerns this uncertainty about reference, because pairing an unfamiliar word with a scene does not uniquely determine what the word means.","children":[]}],"development2-13":[{"text":"**Joint attention** involves **sharing an attentional focus with another**, allowing a child and another person to coordinate which object or event their interaction concerns. The child and adult attend to the same object and coordinate that focus through social information.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze following** emerges around **nine months**, allowing infants to follow the direction of another person’s eyes. Following another person’s gaze supports identifying what that person is referring to, rather than assuming every spoken label concerns whichever object is already most salient to the child.","children":[]}],"development2-14":[{"text":"In **Baldwin and colleagues’ joint attention group**, an **adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with**, coordinating the spoken label with a visibly shared focus. In the **no-joint-attention condition**, a voice behind a screen labeled that object without the visible shared interaction.","children":[]},{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds** learned the novel word in the joint-attention condition but not in the no-joint-attention condition. An object and a spoken label occurred in both, but learning differed with the social coordination of reference.","children":[]}],"development2-15":[{"text":"**Intentional cues from the speaker**, such as **eye gaze direction**, help children narrow the hypothesis space for a new word by identifying what the speaker is trying to refer to. The speaker can be referring to an object different from the one the infant is currently handling.","children":[]},{"text":"Baldwin tested **16–17-month-olds** and **18–19-month-olds** with two labeling arrangements. In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy already occupying the infant’s attention. In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket instead.","children":[]},{"text":"The contrast separates the child’s own current focus from the adult’s intended referent. Tracking gaze provides information about which object the new label is meant to name.","children":[]}],"development2-16":[{"text":"**Gaze direction** can guide word learning by indicating the object a speaker is attending to, supplying information about the intended referent rather than only the child’s current focus. Coordinating a spoken label with the object the speaker is looking at distinguishes that referent from other objects available to the child.","children":[]}],"development2-17":[{"text":"**Intentional cues from the speaker** include **affective and behavioral cues**, so a person’s facial expression and actions can provide information about what a new label refers to. A facial expression can distinguish an object that satisfies the adult’s goal from one that is not the intended target.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Akhtar and colleagues’ study**, infants used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word. Word learning therefore incorporated social evidence about the adult’s response to an object, rather than only the sound’s temporal association with everything present.","children":[]}],"development2-18":[{"text":"**Intentional cues** conveyed through an adult’s emotional reaction can identify the intended referent of a new word, connecting the label to what the adult is responding to. The child can relate the expression accompanying an interaction to the candidate object, using affect as information about the label’s reference.","children":[]}],"development2-19":[{"text":"**Default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation** constrain the set of possible meanings considered when they hear an unfamiliar word used to name something. A default favors one interpretation unless other evidence supports a different one, reducing the number of candidate meanings considered.","children":[]},{"text":"The **whole-object assumption** favors interpreting a new label as the name of an entire object rather than an isolated part, property, or action. This narrows the hypothesis space without requiring every visible aspect of the scene to be tested separately.","children":[]}],"development2-20":[{"text":"Under **mutual exclusivity**, associated with **Markman (1989)**, **as a default, objects can have only one name**, so an already known label influences where a new label is assigned. If a child already knows a label for one object, a novel label is more likely to be assigned to an object without a known name.","children":[]},{"text":"This strategy emerges during the **second half of the second year**. Existing word knowledge constrains new reference because an already named object becomes a less likely candidate for the unfamiliar word.","children":[]}],"development2-21":[{"text":"Under **mutual exclusivity**, a child preferentially assigns a new label to an object without a known name, using existing word knowledge to reduce the competing possibilities for reference. The inference depends on the child’s existing vocabulary and the available alternatives, rather than on the unfamiliar sound alone.","children":[]}],"development2-22":[{"text":"When children produce **only one, two or three words at a time**, the same short utterance can reflect different kinds of organization rather than providing an unambiguous description of their grammatical knowledge. **Menn** distinguishes a set of relevant words, a memorized **formula**, a productive word combination, and a **stable construction** that can differ from an adult-like sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"**“Up!”**, **“Pick you up!”**, and **“Mommy up!”** differ in length, but length alone does not identify the process generating them. A longer expression can be stored as a fixed formula, while a shorter combination can involve selecting and combining elements.","children":[]},{"text":"The interactional context is necessary for interpreting what the child means and how the expression is being used. Even detailed context does not always uniquely distinguish memorization from productive construction.","children":[]}],"development2-23":[{"text":"**Even children at the one-word stage in production are able to use basic transitive word order in comprehension**, so their understanding of sentence structure can exceed the combinations they produce in speech. Children who usually produce one word at a time can already use basic **transitive word order** to interpret who acts on whom.","children":[]},{"text":"In **“The boy is tickling the girl,”** the first noun maps to the agent and the second noun to the patient of tickling. A scene may also contain the girl giggling and both children playing, but the sentence’s noun order selects the participants’ roles in the transitive event.","children":[]},{"text":"The **structure-mapping account** connects a partial sentence representation with a conceptual event representation. Knowledge of the ordered noun positions can support comprehension before the child regularly produces a full sentence with the same structure.","children":[]}],"development2-24":[{"text":"**If children have really learned the transitive structure, they will generalize it to new verbs**, which **Gertner and colleagues (2006)** tested by asking whether two-year-olds understood a sentence containing a novel action word. The sentence **“The duck is gorping the bunny”** was paired with two unfamiliar actions that exchanged which character acted on the other.","children":[]},{"text":"The children looked longer at the event in which the **duck acted on the rabbit**, rather than the reverse. Because gorping was unfamiliar, the intended role relationship had to come from the noun order and transitive construction rather than a previously learned meaning for that verb.","children":[]},{"text":"Instructions occurred during blank-screen intervals and repeated **“The duck is gorping the bunny”** during the test events. The procedure measured preferential looking rather than requiring a spoken sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"Children at this age typically have a **mean length of utterance**, or **MLU**, near **two morphemes**. A production such as **“Duck gorp bunny”** is shorter and less grammatically complete than the sentence they can interpret, separating productive output from receptive structural knowledge.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-01":[],"l1-critical-02":[{"text":"A strict **critical period** has a **discrete, programmed onset and offset**, so the developmental window for learning has a defined opening and closing rather than changing gradually across the lifespan. Learning from the relevant environment is limited to that interval, with no corresponding sensitivity before it opens or after it closes.","children":[]},{"text":"A **sensitive period**, also called an optimal period, permits learning across the lifespan but makes some developmental windows more favorable. Its onset and offset can be gradual and can themselves depend on experience.","children":[]},{"text":"The difference concerns whether later learning is impossible or simply less effective under ordinary conditions. An early advantage together with some later plasticity is compatible with a sensitive period rather than an absolute cutoff.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-03":[{"text":"**Amblyopia** is **poor vision in one eye despite normal peripheral function**, meaning that a lasting deficit in visual performance can remain even when the eye’s current physical functioning is adequate. Abnormal visual input during development can produce a lasting functional deficit even after the original eye problem is corrected.","children":[]},{"text":"**Strabismus** misaligns the eyes, **cataract** obstructs normal light transmission through the lens, and **anisometropia** gives the eyes different refractive properties. Each can deprive one eye of normal, coordinated input during development.","children":[]},{"text":"Restoring the eye’s optical condition does not automatically restore the cortical organization that developed during deprivation. The long-term impairment can persist after the initial physical cause has been treated.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-04":[{"text":"Amblyopia occurs because of **competitive dynamics between cortical inputs**, where the input from each eye competes to influence the brain’s visual representation rather than developing in complete isolation. When one eye supplies abnormal input, the other eye can gain influence over cortical representation that would otherwise support the disadvantaged eye.","children":[]},{"text":"During **monocular deprivation**, early changes include increased movement of dendritic spines after about **2–3 days**. **Spine loss** and degeneration occur around **3–4 days**, while **axon sprouting** and formation of new spines occur after more than a week. These changes alter the connections through which the eyes influence the cortex.","children":[]},{"text":"When normal input returns, the previously deprived eye remains at a **competitive disadvantage**. Ordinary binocular experience can maintain the unequal organization rather than immediately restore its former balance.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-05":[{"text":"**Intermittently patching the good eye** is an amblyopia treatment based on competition, temporarily reducing the stronger eye’s ability to dominate the visual input reaching the brain. The weaker eye then has more opportunity to drive visual processing, allowing experience-dependent plasticity to improve function.","children":[]},{"text":"The treatment is most effective early in development. Its effectiveness decreases with age and it can be ineffective by late childhood, around **ten years** in the developmental comparison. The same alteration of input has different effects depending on when it occurs.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-06":[{"text":"**Long-term, intensive training on specific psychophysical tasks in the affected eye** can improve adult amblyopia, as **Levi and Polat (1996)** reported for both practiced-task performance and broader visual function. Some participants were in their **fifties**, so visual plasticity remained possible well beyond childhood.","children":[]},{"text":"Performance improved over **tens of thousands of training trials**, with periods of faster improvement and periods of less change. The benefits included the practiced task and broader visual function.","children":[]},{"text":"Adult improvement requires a particular kind and intensity of experience. Reduced effectiveness of ordinary late treatment therefore differs from a complete inability of the adult system to change.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-07":[{"text":"**Playing video games enhances visual acuity and visual attention in adult amblyopia**, as reported by **Li, Ngo, Nguyen, Lam, and Levi (2008)**, linking intensive visual experience with improvement beyond the early developmental period. Acuity concerns resolving fine visual detail, while visual attention concerns selecting and responding to relevant visual information.","children":[]},{"text":"The reported improvement occurred in adults with an existing developmental visual deficit, so the affected visual system retained some capacity to change through experience.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-08":[{"text":"A **peripheral disturbance in early experience can lead to lifelong disruption in visual function**, because a temporary deficit can alter the **experience-dependent organization of the brain** during development. The eye’s original physical problem and the later cortical deficit are therefore different stages in the causal sequence.","children":[]},{"text":"Treatment is most effective during a restricted developmental window, but specialized experience can also improve adult function. Amblyopia combines early sensitivity with residual later plasticity, fitting the idea of a **sensitive period**.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-09":[{"text":"**Lenneberg’s early attempts to synthesize developmental neurology with the notion of a language acquisition device** connect brain development with a proposed biologically supported capacity for acquiring language. The hypothesis concerns how the state of the developing brain affects language acquisition.","children":[]},{"text":"**Early language deprivation** provides a different kind of evidence. Neglected children and Deaf children with limited access to sign can encounter a first language late, allowing the consequences of delayed input to be compared with typical early exposure.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-10":[{"text":"In **Biological Foundations of Language (1967)**, **Lenneberg** connected a **stage-like pattern of language development** with maturational milestones and the possible unfolding of a biological program. Words appear before telegraphic phrases, while spontaneous sentences containing several words emerge later.","children":[]},{"text":"The developmental descriptions place repeated sound patterns and early vocal play before a clear word repertoire. Around **twelve months**, repeated sound sequences and emerging words such as mama or dada accompany expanding motor abilities. By about **eighteen months**, a more definite vocabulary coexists with complex babbling and growing understanding.","children":[]},{"text":"The proposed **biological program** concerns an ordered change in the conditions supporting language. Relatively regular maturational milestones were interpreted as evidence that acquisition depends partly on developmental state rather than input quantity alone.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-11":[{"text":"The data on typical brain development were **almost entirely based on post-mortem analyses of tissue**, so Lenneberg’s evidence came mainly from measurements made after death rather than repeated observations of the same living children. Measurements of neuron size increased rapidly in early development, but the available sample had no measurements between roughly **two and eighteen years**.","children":[]},{"text":"Changes in **neuron size** and **packing density** before age two were proposed to make the brain ready for the beginning of language acquisition. Packing density concerns how closely cells occupy the tissue.","children":[]},{"text":"Under this account, the learning window opens after an initial period of rapid neural development. The correspondence between that proposal and the tissue data rests on the ages actually sampled, rather than a continuously observed switch in brain function.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-12":[{"text":"**Recovery from stroke** and severe brain injury differs between children and adults, with children often developing substantially better language outcomes even after extensive damage to language-related tissue. In extreme cases, **hemispherectomy**, removal of a cerebral hemisphere to treat severe seizures, can be followed by substantial language development even when the left hemisphere is removed early.","children":[]},{"text":"In the reported historical compilation, many operations before the teens left speech unaffected or improved. A puberty case involved slow improvement, while adult left-hemisphere operations were associated with permanent aphasia. Adult right-hemisphere operations had a different language outcome.","children":[]},{"text":"Lenneberg linked this age difference to **cerebral dominance**, the developing specialization of the hemispheres. An early, less fixed organization was proposed to permit more reassignment of language functions than the adult organization.","children":[]},{"text":"In the hypothesis, development of adult-like dominance contributes to closing the favorable learning window. The account connects developmental state with the different consequences of losing comparable brain tissue at different ages.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-13":[{"text":"**Perinatal strokes** occur during the **prenatal period or within 28 days after birth**, so the injury can affect the developing brain before birth or during the first weeks of life. Children with these injuries generally have better language outcomes than adults with similar damage, but their development is not always unaffected.","children":[]},{"text":"Early language deficits occur after both **left- and right-hemisphere damage**. Some early problems can be greater after right-hemisphere injury, rather than following a simple adult-like localization pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"Later performance often remains in the **low-normal range**. The trajectory can therefore involve a persistent delay rather than complete catch-up, even when the outcome is much better than after comparable adult injury.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-14":[{"text":"**Effects of early deprivation** were studied in **Genie**, described by **Curtiss and colleagues (1974)**, whose severe neglect included very limited access to language and ordinary social interaction. She was first studied at **thirteen years and seven months**, after confinement beginning at about **twenty months**.","children":[]},{"text":"She had been physically restrained, received minimal care, and was punished for making sounds. Ordinary interaction and access to spoken language were severely restricted, so her early experience involved deprivation in several domains at once.","children":[]},{"text":"On admission she was malnourished and did not speak, with substantial difficulties in everyday physical functioning. Her later language acquisition occurred after this extended history of limited input and abuse.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-15":[{"text":"During the **four years that her language was observed**, Genie acquired **comprehension of hundreds of words**, **some aspects of morphology**, and **some prepositions**, extending her understanding beyond a small set of object labels. **Plural morphology** was a relative strength because word forms could distinguish one item from more than one.","children":[]},{"text":"Preposition testing required manipulating buttons and boxes to follow instructions such as **“Put the red box behind the blue box.”** The task measured the spatial relationship conveyed by the word, rather than requiring a spoken definition.","children":[]},{"text":"Correct responses exceeded incorrect responses for **on, in, over, under, in front of, in back of, next to, and behind**. For example, behind received **12 correct responses and 5 incorrect responses**, while in received **37 correct and 4 incorrect responses**. Her understanding therefore extended beyond isolated object names to some relationships between objects.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-16":[{"text":"**Genie’s production lagged severely behind her comprehension**, so what she could understand was substantially more developed than what she could readily express through spoken language. Some receptive advantage is typical, but her history also included punishment for vocalizing, which could affect how readily knowledge appeared in speech.","children":[]},{"text":"**Grammar** developed more slowly than vocabulary, and conjunction testing required distinguishing the meanings of and and or. In that testing, she had to respond to requests such as **“Show me the fork and pencil,” “Show me the crayon or the knife,”** and **“Show me either the spoon or the crayon.”**","children":[]},{"text":"She initially treated or much like and, and although she later differentiated the words, she did not reliably understand the either/or relation. For the reported period, **and** sentences received **100 correct and 3 incorrect** responses, while **or** sentences received **7 correct, 51 incorrect, and 6 unanalyzable** responses. The difficulty concerned a grammatical meaning relationship despite substantial word learning.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-17":[{"text":"The **disparity between Genie’s vocabulary development and grammatical development** was substantial, with far more progress in word knowledge than in grammar during the period when her learning was observed. That disparity has been interpreted as evidence that late first-language learning affects grammar especially strongly.","children":[]},{"text":"Her case also involved possible learning difficulties and the developmental effects of neglect and abuse. Those factors co-occurred with delayed language exposure, so the language pattern cannot be assigned to age alone.","children":[]},{"text":"After **four years**, the funding to study Genie was withdrawn, so her learning over a longer period was not measured within that observation program. Interpretations of her vocabulary and grammar development are therefore based on the course of learning actually recorded during those four years.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-18":[{"text":"**Mayberry, Lock, and Kazmi (2002)** compared children encountering ASL for the first time with children who **went deaf after initially learning a spoken language**, separating delayed L1 from later L2 learning. Deaf children who first encountered **American Sign Language** at **9–15 years** had persistent difficulty, even when their upbringing was otherwise typical.","children":[]},{"text":"Children who became deaf after first learning a spoken language performed better with later ASL. A late first language and a late second language therefore produced different outcomes despite both involving later sign exposure.","children":[]},{"text":"In adulthood, the no-early-language group performed worse on recalling complex ASL sentences than the early-spoken-language group. In English grammaticality testing, groups with an early signed or spoken language also outperformed the group without early language. The advantage concerns early **linguistic experience**, rather than requiring that the early and later language use the same modality.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-19":[{"text":"**Recovery from stroke and hemispherectomy looks very different in childhood versus adulthood**, because the developing system and the adult system have different language outcomes after extensive injury or removal of a hemisphere. The developing system can support language under conditions that produce much more severe and persistent impairment later.","children":[]},{"text":"Lack of input does not appear to keep a first-language learning window open indefinitely. Delayed first-language access can be followed by lasting difficulty even after language input becomes available.","children":[]},{"text":"The evidence comes from unusual histories involving injury or deprivation, with other developmental factors present. Human language also lacks an equivalent experimental animal model in which the relevant capacity can be selectively removed and tested, so biological explanations must fit the observed cases rather than depend on such manipulations.","children":[]}],"l1-critical-20":[],"l1-critical-21":[],"l2-learning-01":[],"l2-learning-02":[{"text":"**Adults initially learn more quickly than children**, so a later learner can acquire the early vocabulary and structures of a second language faster even though eventual native-like attainment is less common. They already have a first language and more developed general cognitive skills, which support the early acquisition of words and structures. A child using about twenty words at eighteen months has a very different starting point from an adult beginning language instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"**Ultimate attainment** concerns the level eventually reached rather than the initial learning rate. Adults are less likely to reach fully native-like performance despite their early speed advantage, and transfer from the first language can contribute to that difference.","children":[]},{"text":"Early development includes growing understanding of spatial relationships, pretend play, pointing, simple questions, and vocal patterns. These abilities are still developing alongside the child’s language, whereas adult learners can use many already established skills.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-03":[{"text":"Some **language milestones are actually general conceptual abilities**, such as the ability to deal with **spatial relations between objects or event representations**, rather than knowledge of a particular language alone. Knowing the concepts of in and out is different from learning the word forms used to express them.","children":[]},{"text":"A later second-language learner already possesses many of those concepts. Learning can concentrate on how the new language represents relationships that are already understood, while the existing first language also influences which forms and patterns are readily acquired.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-04":[{"text":"**Transfer** is **the use of native language L1 processes and representations for a second language L2**, bringing already established ways of perceiving, representing, and using language into the new learning situation. An established way of interpreting sounds, organizing grammar, or accessing words can be recruited during the new language.","children":[]},{"text":"Transfer is **positive** when that organization fits the L2 requirement and **negative** when it supports an inappropriate interpretation. The effect depends on the relationship between the languages rather than the existence of prior language knowledge alone.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-05":[{"text":"**Native English speakers tend to use the alveolar category to interpret both dental and retroflex consonants** in Hindi, treating sounds that Hindi distinguishes as members of one familiar English category. Dental articulation involves the teeth, alveolar articulation the ridge behind them, and retroflex articulation a farther-back tongue configuration.","children":[]},{"text":"Hindi minimal pairs distinguish words through these place differences, including pairs associated with meanings such as pressure versus a kind of fruit, and a saw versus crooked. For a native listener, changing the consonant can identify a different word.","children":[]},{"text":"English experience creates **acquired similarity** among sounds assigned to the same category. Applying that category to both Hindi consonants reduces their distinctiveness, so L1 organization can make the required L2 contrast harder to learn.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-06":[{"text":"**Classifiers are a somewhat unusual grammatical feature cross-linguistically**, and a classifier is a grammatical form selected in relation to a noun. Chinese nouns require different classifiers in expressions where an English speaker would use phrases such as **a chair, that dog, the car,** or **one book** without making the same classifier choice.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gè** is a general classifier, **bǎ** is used with objects having handles, and **běn** with books and related objects. **Kuài** is used with pieces, **liàng** with wheeled vehicles, and **tái** with devices or gadgets.","children":[]},{"text":"**Xiē** expresses an indeterminate plural amount, **zhāng** is used with flat objects, and **zhī** with long thin objects. The relevant semantic or shape properties help determine which grammatical form accompanies the noun.","children":[]},{"text":"English-speaking learners have not normally used those distinctions to select comparable forms in English. Learning the Chinese system therefore requires attending to cues that their established grammar allowed them to ignore.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-07":[{"text":"**Grammatical gender is different from social or cultural gender**, because it classifies nouns grammatically rather than stating that the objects named by those nouns are socially male or female. A book does not become male because **livre** is grammatically masculine, and liberty does not become female because **liberté** is grammatically feminine.","children":[]},{"text":"Gender affects **agreement** with pronouns, adjectives, and other grammatical forms. The learner must coordinate these forms with the noun’s class even when the referent supplies no obvious gender cue.","children":[]},{"text":"French near-synonyms can differ in grammatical gender, including **auto/voiture, rivière/fleuve,** and **vélo/bicyclette**. Similar meanings therefore do not guarantee the same agreement pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"Word endings can provide statistical cues, including masculine-associated **-age, -r, -t, -sme** and feminine-associated **-ie, -ion, -ité/-tié, -ance, -me, -mme, -lle** in the provided examples. These are form-based tendencies represented by example words, rather than a property of the objects’ social identities.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-08":[{"text":"**Vocabulary learning in some L1-L2 situations is aided by the similarity of words across languages**, making a new form easier to associate with familiar word knowledge in L1. **Cognates** resemble one another because of shared historical origin, while **loan words** enter a language through borrowing.","children":[]},{"text":"French **auto, rivière,** and **bicyclette** resemble familiar vocabulary for a car, river, and bicycle. Japanese **コロッケ, korokke**, names a croquette, and **モノレール, monoreru**, names a monorail through adapted borrowed forms.","children":[]},{"text":"An existing form–meaning connection can provide support for the new label. Positive transfer therefore gives the learner a partially familiar route into some L2 words.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-09":[{"text":"**Not all similar-sounding words are cognates**, so relying on a familiar L1 form can produce a **false-friend error** when the similar L2 word has a different meaning. Spanish **embarazado** concerns pregnancy rather than the English meaning of embarrassed, so **“Arnold esta embarazado”** does not express ordinary embarrassment.","children":[]},{"text":"Strong activation of a similar L1 word can encourage an incorrect meaning assignment. Form resemblance facilitates some vocabulary learning but can also mislead when the corresponding meanings do not match.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-10":[{"text":"**De Groot and Keijzer (2000)** used **paired associate learning of made-up words**, varying their similarity to familiar Dutch associates to test how existing word forms affect new vocabulary learning. Participants learned pairs of Dutch words and made-up associates, then returned for a retention test a week later.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gitaar–gettar**, meaning guitar, was **concrete and cognate-like**, while **tijger–duunze**, meaning tiger, was concrete and non-cognate. **Domein–domaan**, meaning domain, was **abstract and cognate-like**, while **herstel–groop**, meaning repair, was abstract and non-cognate.","children":[]},{"text":"**Concreteness** distinguishes more directly imaginable referents from abstract meanings, and cognate-like status distinguishes similar from dissimilar forms. Crossing the factors lets form similarity be compared within both kinds of meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"Learning the pair requires establishing which unfamiliar form belongs with the known Dutch word. A cognate-like associate supplies partial form overlap that can support encoding and later retrieval.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-11":[{"text":"**Cognates are learned more rapidly and also remembered more easily**, with cognate-like associates benefiting both during initial paired-associate learning and after a delay without additional training. For concrete items, recall increased from **69.7% versus 43.7%** in Session 1 to **93.4% versus 92.0%** in Session 3. The non-cognate group could approach the cognate group with repeated learning.","children":[]},{"text":"After a week without training, **Session 4** concrete recall was **78.4% for cognates** and **69.0% for non-cognates**. Abstract-item recall was **68.4% versus 36.0%**, compared with **86.0% versus 72.7%** immediately before the delay.","children":[]},{"text":"Response times also favored cognates, with faster retrieval of the similar associates. Session 4 concrete responses averaged **1,833 versus 2,597 milliseconds**, and abstract responses **2,333 versus 3,412 milliseconds**.","children":[]},{"text":"Similarity therefore contributed to learning and retrieval from the earliest sessions, as well as to performance after the delay. The advantage was not restricted to an already proficient L2 vocabulary.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-12":[{"text":"Testing **receptive vocabulary** measures **how easily participants could produce the Dutch word given its associate**, supplying the unfamiliar associate and requiring a response in the already known language. Similarity can make that direction relatively easy because the unfamiliar form already resembles a familiar word.","children":[]},{"text":"The concrete cognate materials include **broer–breur, brood–brodde, gitaar–gettar, jongen–jange, lepel–lippel, lichaam–lechem, naald–nadel, paraplu–parredu, rivier–riffer, schaar–skare, stier–strire, trein–trien, varken–vark, viool–vialo,** and **vrouw–vrauk**. Their resemblance provides a cue to the intended familiar partner.","children":[]},{"text":"**Productive or expressive testing** reverses the requirement and asks for the learned unfamiliar form. A correct response then requires retrieving the associate itself, so learning cannot be explained only by recognizing or guessing a familiar meaning from a similar-looking input.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-13":[{"text":"In the **productive test**, delayed recall after one week fell from **92.7% to 73.7% for concrete items** and from **79.3% to 52.2% for abstract items**, even though the corresponding immediate-recall scores had been high. Cognate recall decreased from **89.7% to 73.3%**, while non-cognate recall decreased from **82.3% to 52.5%**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Receptive recall** was higher overall, decreasing from **97.7% to 88.9%** for concrete items and **91.5% to 64.9%** for abstract items. Cognates decreased from **98.5% to 90.7%**, while non-cognates decreased from **90.7% to 63.0%**.","children":[]},{"text":"Producing a new form and recognizing its familiar partner therefore place different demands on the learned association. Better retention for cognates occurs in both directions, including the direction that requires the unfamiliar form to be generated.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-14":[{"text":"Whether **similar L1 words are always activated when processing L2** concerns whether recognition in the second language partly activates first-language candidates even when those candidates are not permitted responses. Cross-language competition concerns this unintended activation rather than a deliberate translation decision.","children":[]},{"text":"**Marian and Spivey (2003)** tested that possibility with a visual-world task. Looking toward an object whose L1 name resembles the spoken L2 target provides a behavioral measure of the competing representation.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-15":[{"text":"**Eye movements can tell us about competition between possible responses participants are considering**, tracking which word candidates attract attention while recognition is still unfolding rather than only recording the final choice. In **McMurray and colleagues’ task**, the display contained **bear, pear, lamp,** and **ship**, while the initial stop varied along a /b/–/p/ continuum.","children":[]},{"text":"Bear and pear attracted different amounts of competitor looking depending on distance from the phoneme boundary. An eventually correct click therefore coexisted with partial activation of a competing word, which was measurable before the final response.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-16":[{"text":"The **cohort effect** involves competition from a word that shares the beginning of a spoken target, so several words can receive activation before the later sounds distinguish them. In the **Allopenna** task, **beaker** and **beetle** share an onset, **speaker** shares the ending with beaker, and **carriage** is unrelated.","children":[]},{"text":"Target and cohort looks increase early, before the continuing input differentiates their forms. The target then gains more looking while cohort looking declines, and rhyme competition follows a different time course.","children":[]},{"text":"Competition is therefore sensitive to when overlapping information occurs within a spoken word. The final target response can conceal alternatives that were active earlier during the same recognition event.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-17":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey examined the cohort effect with cross-language competitors**, testing whether Russian-English bilinguals consider objects whose names overlap with the target in the other language. The Russian word **спички, spichki**, meaning matches, begins similarly to English **speaker**. During an English instruction naming speaker, the matches object can therefore receive support from its Russian name.","children":[]},{"text":"The English within-language competitor **spear** shares the target’s onset as well. Displays can include the within-language competitor, the Russian-name competitor, or both, separating the sources of overlap.","children":[]},{"text":"Other cross-language target relations include **boot** with Russian **buben**, a tambourine, **chair** with **cherepaha**, a turtle, and **marker** with **marka**, a stamp. A look toward such an object during English processing indicates activation tied to the other language’s form rather than its English label.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-18":[{"text":"During **L2 spoken word recognition**, **when only one competitor was present, participants made eye movements to both within- and between-language competitors to the same extent**, about **18% of trials** for each competitor type. Both within-language and between-language overlap therefore affected which objects participants looked at during recognition.","children":[]},{"text":"When both competitors were present, within-language looks occurred on about **19%** of trials and between-language looks on about **13%**. The control filler attracted looks on about **7%** of trials.","children":[]},{"text":"Within-language competition was stronger when the alternatives competed together, but the L1-name competitor still attracted substantially more looking than an unrelated control. Activation during L2 recognition therefore extended across language membership.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-19":[{"text":"During **L1 spoken word recognition in Russian**, a **within-language competitor** attracted looks on about **14% of trials when alone** and **13% when both competitors were present**, exceeding looks to unrelated control objects. Between-language English competitors attracted about **8%**, compared with a control rate near **5%**.","children":[]},{"text":"The small difference between the English-name competitor and control was not statistically significant. The reported cross-language effect was therefore asymmetric, with clearer L1 competition during L2 processing than L2 competition during L1 processing.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-20":[{"text":"**The Revised Hierarchical Model of Kroll and Stewart** explains **L1-L2 interactions in terms of spreading activation**, linking the two lexical systems with shared concepts through connections of unequal strength. A lexical link joins word representations, while a conceptual link connects a word with meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"The **L1 lexicon** is proposed to be larger and more strongly connected to concepts. The developing **L2 lexicon** has stronger lexical connections toward L1 than L1 has toward L2, reflecting the translation direction emphasized during learning.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation therefore spreads more readily from an L2 word to its L1 counterpart than in the reverse direction. Unequal link strengths produce unequal interaction even when both languages belong to the same person.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-21":[{"text":"During the **Marian and Spivey task in L2**, the visible matches object activates its concept, and **the concept for matches partially activates the Russian word for matches**, creating a possible competing word candidate. Activation then feeds back from the Russian word into the conceptual representation.","children":[]},{"text":"The strengthened matches concept contributes to eye movements toward that object, creating a competing response to the English target. Lexical and conceptual activity can therefore reinforce one another even when the candidate belongs to the task-irrelevant language.","children":[]},{"text":"In an L1 task, the links involving the weaker L2 system may be insufficient to create equally reliable interference. The model explains the asymmetry through the strength and direction of the participating connections.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-22":[{"text":"**Balanced bilinguals do not look like two monolinguals in one**, because similarly strong conceptual links can keep **competitive dynamics and inhibition** engaged across both languages during recognition. Equal proficiency does not require the languages to function as isolated systems.","children":[]},{"text":"The prediction differs from treating a bilingual as **“two monolinguals in one.”** Activation can spread across shared concepts and both lexical systems, creating control demands even when the intended response is in only one language.","children":[]},{"text":"This interaction motivated research on performance in **nonlinguistic cognitive-control tasks**. The model’s immediate claim concerns the competition and inhibition produced by its connected language representations.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-23":[{"text":"A person can **begin learning L1**, add L2, and continue learning and using L1, so the first language keeps receiving input and practice after the second language has been introduced. Both languages continue to receive activation after the second language is introduced.","children":[]},{"text":"Another history involves adding L2 and then stopping L1 use. The first language then loses ongoing input and practice, so later performance can depend on both the age of learning and the subsequent pattern of use.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-24":[{"text":"**Adults ages 20–32 who had been adopted into French homes from Korean orphanages between the ages of 3 and 8** were tested by **Pallier and colleagues** for evidence of retained Korean after the change in their language environment. Their early language experience differed from their later French-dominant environment.","children":[]},{"text":"The study concerned **residual knowledge of Korean**, meaning information retained from the earlier exposure. Current language performance could be compared with that of French speakers who lacked the same childhood history.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-25":[{"text":"The **Korean three-way stop contrast** distinguishes three stop-consonant categories at the same place of articulation, so the distinction concerns how the stop is produced rather than where the closure occurs. **Aspirated, plain, and tense** stops identify different words rather than variants of a single interchangeable sound.","children":[]},{"text":"The bilabial series includes forms meaning **grass, fire,** and **horn**. The dental series includes **mask, moon,** and **daughter**, while the velar series includes **nose, music,** and **exactly** in the provided materials.","children":[]},{"text":"The place of articulation is shared within each series, while the laryngeal contrast differs. Retaining Korean phonetic knowledge would therefore involve separating these categories rather than only distinguishing a lip sound from a tongue sound.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-26":[{"text":"**Ventureyra and colleagues found that Korean adoptees in France were no better than monolingual French speakers at these contrasts**, comparing adoptees with native French and Korean listeners on Korean phonetic distinctions. The task included **plain–tense, plain–aspirated,** and **tense–aspirated** stop contrasts, as well as vowel differences and same trials.","children":[]},{"text":"Native Korean listeners detected the Korean consonantal distinctions much more successfully. Adoptees performed much like the monolingual French group on those contrasts, despite their earlier Korean exposure.","children":[]},{"text":"The vowel and same-trial conditions differed from the difficult Korean stop contrasts, so poor consonant performance was not simply an inability to make any auditory judgment. The reported test detected no phonetic advantage for adoptees over French controls.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-27":[{"text":"**Pallier and colleagues looked for any remaining trace of Korean** through several behavioral tasks, testing whether adoptees retained detectable knowledge that ordinary current language use might fail to reveal. In language identification, adoptees judged whether sentences were Korean rather than Japanese, Polish, Swedish, or Wolof. Their responses did not provide a reliable advantage over monolingual French listeners.","children":[]},{"text":"In **word recognition**, participants selected Korean translations of French words. In **speech-segment detection**, they judged whether a short excerpt had occurred in a recorded sentence. Neither task produced a dependable advantage for the adoptees over participants without the same early Korean experience.","children":[]},{"text":"These tasks measured different possible remnants, including language familiarity, lexical knowledge, and sensitivity to spoken sequences. Across them, early Korean experience did not produce a detectable current behavioral benefit under the tested conditions.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-28":[{"text":"Listening to **French activated the classical language network, including superior temporal and inferior frontal regions**, in the adoptees, relating their current dominant language to the broad network associated with language processing. Their current dominant language therefore recruited the broad network associated with language processing.","children":[]},{"text":"Comparisons of French with **Polish, Japanese,** and **Korean** assessed responses to the familiar language relative to other speech. Subtracting the Korean response from the French response did not reveal the distinguishing group difference expected from readily accessible residual Korean processing.","children":[]},{"text":"The reported imaging comparison therefore did not detect a clear retained Korean advantage, alongside the absence of an advantage in the behavioral measures.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-29":[{"text":"**L1-L2 interactions can be understood in terms of competitive dynamics**, with an L1 represented as **a set of connections within and between different brain regions** that depend partly on continued activation. If L1 connections are no longer activated, L2-related patterns can become dominant in the same broad system.","children":[]},{"text":"The proposed mechanism resembles experience-dependent competition between visual inputs. Reduced activation changes which connections remain effective, so replacement concerns a changing balance of supported pathways rather than moving a complete language object between storage locations.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-30":[{"text":"**Traces of L1 left behind after a language change can be salvaged through later experience**, even when the language no longer produces an obvious advantage in tests of current performance. **Residual knowledge** means a surviving influence of earlier experience, even when ordinary access to that language is weak.","children":[]},{"text":"**Relearning** can make such an influence behaviorally accessible by activating the relevant forms again. Present inability and later learning consequences are therefore different measurements of what remains from an early language history.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-31":[{"text":"**Au and colleagues studied undergraduate students who overheard Spanish frequently as children but never learned to speak the language**, separating early auditory exposure from a history of active childhood speaking. Participants began classroom Spanish around **age fourteen** and were tested after a **second-year college Spanish course**.","children":[]},{"text":"Childhood overhearers had more native-like phonetic performance than typical late learners. For word-initial voiceless stops, mean VOT was about **19.6 milliseconds** for overhearers and **19.3 milliseconds** for native speakers, compared with **36.2 milliseconds** for typical late learners.","children":[]},{"text":"Accent ratings and rates of lenition also favored the overhearers on several measures, although their childhood slang scores remained well below native performance. Earlier hearing could support aspects of later pronunciation without producing a fully native-like vocabulary or grammar.","children":[]},{"text":"In morphology and grammaticality testing, overhearers and typical late learners were much closer than either group was to native speakers. The consequences of early exposure therefore differed across language components rather than improving every measure equally.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-32":[{"text":"**If L1 is removed from the environment entirely, L2 can appear to completely replace it**, so current behavior may no longer reveal an obvious role for the earlier language. **Residual knowledge** can nevertheless become accessible through subsequent learning.","children":[]},{"text":"Korean-adoptee performance concerns current accessibility after a major language shift, while childhood-overhearer performance includes outcomes after renewed formal instruction. Those histories measure different opportunities for early experience to influence performance.","children":[]}],"l2-learning-33":[{"text":"**Korean is rarely heard or encountered in France, unlike Spanish in Los Angeles**, so these language histories differ in how much incidental exposure can continue after the main language environment changes. A heritage language spoken by family members can remain socially available even when the learner does not actively speak it.","children":[]},{"text":"The language pairs also differ in **cognates** and **shared writing systems**, which can support learning in their own right. A benefit after renewed instruction can therefore reflect several interacting aspects of the earlier and later environments.","children":[]},{"text":"Language histories differ in the duration, form, and social context of exposure. Comparing residual knowledge across populations requires keeping those differences distinct from the mere fact that both groups encountered an early language.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-01":[],"l2-sensitive-02":[{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967)** proposed that the sensitive period has to do with **the development of cerebral dominance, or developmental changes in language laterality**, linking favorable conditions for language acquisition with increasing hemispheric specialization. Developmental changes in language laterality were connected with changes in the capacity to acquire language.","children":[]},{"text":"The proposal combined information about neural development and language milestones with the greater resilience of children after brain injury or surgery. Age-related recovery differences supplied evidence that the language system’s organization changes across development.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-03":[{"text":"Much of what is known about **L1 sensitive periods** comes from **idiosyncratic cases or relatively rare phenomena**, including rare injuries or unusual histories of language deprivation. Those cases combine language timing with other developmental circumstances.","children":[]},{"text":"**Second-language learning** occurs at many ages in a much larger population. Variation in eventual L2 success can therefore be compared with the age at which learning began, providing a different source of evidence about developmental constraints.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-04":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport** tested the English proficiency attained by **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers** who had arrived in the United States between **ages three and thirty-nine**, comparing the long-term outcomes of learners with different arrival ages. At testing, they had lived there for **three to twenty-six years**.","children":[]},{"text":"The participants completed a **grammaticality judgment task** covering several English structures. The study compared eventual performance with age of arrival rather than measuring only the speed of the first weeks of instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals generally performed more accurately, with a strong age relationship before puberty. Later arrivals performed less accurately overall but varied considerably, and their scores were not tightly ordered by their arrival ages.","children":[]},{"text":"The reported age effect was not accounted for by the measured differences in English experience, motivation, self-consciousness, or American identification. Johnson and Newport interpreted the pattern as evidence that maturational state influences L2 acquisition.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-05":[{"text":"In the **grammaticality judgment test**, or **GJT**, participants **listened to recorded sentences and simply had to say whether each sentence was grammatical or not**, making an explicit judgment about each spoken item. Johnson and Newport’s task was **untimed**, allowing a response after the sentence rather than demanding rapid conversational use.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The farmer bought two pigs at the market”** contrasts with **“two pig”** and tests plural marking. **“The little boy is speaking to a policeman”** contrasts with **“is speak”** and tests the required verb form.","children":[]},{"text":"**“Tom is reading a book in the bathtub”** contrasts with a missing article, **“reading book.”** **“The boys are going to the zoo”** contrasts with **“A boys,”** while **“Larry went home”** contrasts with the inappropriate **“went the home.”** These items test grammatical requirements rather than whether the event itself is plausible.","children":[]},{"text":"**“When will Sam fix his car?”** contrasts with **“When Sam will fix his car?”**, and **“What do they sell at the corner store?”** contrasts with **“What they sell at the corner store?”** The question pairs test auxiliary placement and the forms required to construct an English question.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-06":[{"text":"For learners arriving at approximately **3–15 years**, **items correct** decreases as **age of arrival**, or **AOA**, increases, producing a strong downward relationship between arrival age and grammaticality accuracy. The reported correlation is about **−0.87**, a strong negative relationship.","children":[]},{"text":"For arrivals aged approximately **17–39**, scores are lower on average but widely dispersed. The correlation is about **−0.16**, so an older arrival within that range does not reliably predict a lower score than every younger adult arrival.","children":[]},{"text":"The pattern combines an early age gradient with later variability. It is different from a single equally strong linear decline across the entire age range.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-07":[{"text":"**Early learners up to about 8 are often near-perfect (native-like)**, achieving very high grammaticality accuracy when their arrival and substantial English exposure begin during early childhood. Among later childhood arrivals, increasing AOA is associated with lower accuracy.","children":[]},{"text":"Adult-arrival scores vary substantially without a comparably strong ordering by age. The **nearly random distribution** describes the weak relationship between adult arrival age and individual accuracy, rather than claiming that every late learner answers each question randomly.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-08":[{"text":"For **Johnson and Newport**, the **dependence of ultimate attainment on AOA is consistent with a loss of language learning ability over development**, connecting lower childhood-arrival performance with a proposed developmental change in learning capacity. The timing does not align perfectly with Lenneberg’s original formulation, which relied on sparse information between early childhood and adolescence.","children":[]},{"text":"They interpret the wide variation among late learners as increasing dependence on **individual learning abilities** after the favorable period closes. Under this account, later success relies less on the usual early acquisition processes and more on differences between people.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-09":[{"text":"Under **the exercise hypothesis**, **early in life humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages**, and that capacity disappears or declines with maturation if it is not exercised during the favorable period. If exercised during the favorable period, that capacity remains available for additional language learning throughout life.","children":[]},{"text":"The **maturational-state hypothesis** also proposes an early superior capacity, but its decline follows maturation even when language was learned successfully during childhood. Early use does not permanently preserve the same learning state.","children":[]},{"text":"The hypotheses differ in what they predict for a person who already has an early L1. Under exercise, prior use protects later language learning, whereas under the maturational-state account, an established L1 can coexist with reduced capacity for learning a later language.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-10":[{"text":"A judgment task can draw on **metalinguistic or explicit knowledge** about rules, whereas **implicit knowledge** supports language use without deliberate explanation of the rule. In **Ellis’s distinction (2005)**, it involves procedural knowledge accessed automatically during fluent performance and is difficult to verbalize directly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Explicit knowledge** is consciously available knowledge about grammatical rules and fragments. It is accessed through controlled processing, can be stated in words, and can be learned at any age in the theoretical comparison.","children":[]},{"text":"An **untimed grammaticality judgment** can draw on explicit or **metalinguistic knowledge**, meaning conscious knowledge about language itself. A correct judgment may therefore reflect deliberation about a taught rule rather than the automatic process used in fluent speech.","children":[]},{"text":"The theoretical categories also differ in how systematically their knowledge is represented and when learning is possible. The proposed critical-period restriction concerns implicit acquisition in the table, while explicit learning remains possible across ages.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-11":[{"text":"**The task demands of the untimed GJT** can make performance a better measure of **explicit learning of metalinguistic knowledge** than of the processes used in fluent language use. The untimed task removes time pressure, focuses attention on form, and allows explicit rule use, while imitation and oral narrative emphasize meaning under more pressured conditions.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Ellis (2005)**, years of formal instruction are associated with performance on the **untimed judgment of ungrammatical items**, with a reported correlation around **0.27**. Starting age has a significant negative association with the timed GJT, around **−0.23**, but little association with the untimed judgments in the reported comparisons.","children":[]},{"text":"Task design therefore changes which aspect of language knowledge is being measured. A form-focused judgment made with unlimited time can reflect explicit education differently from a response made under the demands of fluent use.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-12":[{"text":"**Languages that are typologically more similar can benefit more from transfer**, because familiar L1 structures and representations may supply more useful support for learning and using the L2. Greater **typological similarity**, meaning similarity in how languages organize linguistic forms, can provide more useful overlap for learning.","children":[]},{"text":"Johnson and Newport tested **Chinese- and Korean-speaking learners of English**. A learner whose first language supplies more similar grammatical distinctions or familiar forms can approach English with different transfer support.","children":[]},{"text":"Cross-language vocabulary resemblance, including cognates and borrowed forms, provides one kind of overlap. A language-pair comparison must distinguish an effect of learning age from differences in what the L1 already makes available.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-13":[{"text":"**Not all aspects of the grammar are equally difficult**, so learners can judge some English constructions accurately while making frequent errors on others that place different demands on their knowledge. **Progressive -ing** and **word order** are relatively easy, while **determiners** and **plural marking** produce many more errors for some later-arriving groups.","children":[]},{"text":"The tested rule categories also include **verb subcategorization, past tense, pronouns, particles, auxiliaries, third-person singular marking, wh-questions,** and **yes/no questions**. Subcategorization concerns which complements a verb permits, while particles and auxiliaries contribute requirements that can differ from those in the learner’s L1.","children":[]},{"text":"Some difficulty patterns resemble early development, but others are compatible with **transfer**. Chinese does not supply the same determiner system as English, while questions that develop late in young children can be relatively easy for older L2 learners. Age does not affect every construction in one uniform way.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-14":[{"text":"**Birdsong and Molis (2001)** compared **Spanish speakers learning English** with the age-of-arrival pattern for **Chinese and Korean speakers learning English**, changing the language pairing while examining grammaticality attainment. The relationship between AOA and grammaticality performance differed across these populations.","children":[]},{"text":"In the Spanish-speaking sample, early-arrival scores remained high without the same strong childhood gradient, with a reported correlation around **−0.23**. Among late arrivals, scores declined more strongly with AOA, with a correlation around **−0.69**.","children":[]},{"text":"That differs from the Johnson–Newport pattern of a strong childhood relationship and weak adult relationship. A fixed maturational cutoff alone does not explain why changing the language pairing changes the shape of the age–performance association.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-15":[{"text":"**Overall, L2 attainment is lower for older learners**, but the measured outcome depends on **what constructions are tested, how they are tested, and language pairings**, rather than reflecting one uniform effect across every task and population. A grammaticality score is therefore a particular measurement of performance rather than a complete description of all language abilities.","children":[]},{"text":"**Age of acquisition** is intertwined with the amount and quality of experience, while **transfer** changes which parts of the L2 are already supported by the L1. **Competence** and **performance** also differ, because a task can permit deliberate rule knowledge or impose time pressure on its use.","children":[]},{"text":"An explanation of later learning must distinguish these contributions to the measured outcome. Different combinations can produce different age relationships even when the general population pattern favors earlier learning.","children":[]}],"l2-sensitive-16":[],"review-1":[],"review-2":[{"text":"A **speech continuum** varies a controlled acoustic property across a sequence of stimuli, allowing equally sized physical changes to be compared with listeners’ labeling and discrimination responses. F1 is held constant while the onset frequency of F2 changes linearly, so each adjacent pair differs by the same programmed amount.","children":[]},{"text":"At one end, F2 begins below its steady-state value and rises into the vowel. As the onset frequency increases, the transition becomes flatter and eventually falls from above the steady-state value. The stimuli remain evenly spaced acoustically even if listeners assign them different phoneme labels.","children":[]},{"text":"Equal spacing allows a within-category pair to be compared with a boundary-crossing pair of the same physical separation. A difference in discrimination between those pairs concerns perceptual organization rather than a larger programmed frequency change.","children":[]}],"review-3":[{"text":"In a simple **ideal observer** model assuming perception is **non-categorical**, categorization would get **“blurry” as you move away from category prototypes**, with neighboring categories becoming increasingly plausible. The endpoints correspond to /b/ and /g/, with /d/ in the middle.","children":[]},{"text":"A prototype is a clear representative of a category. Moving away from it makes the stimulus progressively less characteristic of that category and more characteristic of a neighboring category. Under this prediction, stimulus 2 would receive about **75% /b/** and **25% /d/** responses.","children":[]},{"text":"Labeling remains a choice among categories, but uncertainty changes smoothly between them. The predicted /b/ response declines gradually as /d/ responses increase, followed by a gradual exchange between /d/ and /g/ responses.","children":[]}],"review-4":[{"text":"In an ideal observer model assuming perception is **linear**, discrimination performance should be **independent of labeling**, so equally separated F2-onset values should be equally discriminable across the continuum. A two-step pair such as 1 versus 3 has the same acoustic separation as 5 versus 7.","children":[]},{"text":"In a **labeling task**, a listener assigns a category such as /b/, /d/, or /g/. In a **discrimination task**, the listener judges whether two stimuli are the same or different. These tasks ask for different responses even when they use the same acoustic continuum.","children":[]},{"text":"The linear account predicts approximately constant discrimination accuracy across equal-distance pairs. Labeling probabilities can still rise and fall across prototypes without producing corresponding peaks in discrimination.","children":[]}],"review-5":[{"text":"**Categorical perception** in **Liberman and colleagues’ results** includes abrupt changes in phoneme labeling, although the stimuli themselves progress through evenly spaced acoustic changes. Early stimuli receive mainly /b/ responses, middle stimuli mainly /d/ responses, and late stimuli mainly /g/ responses, even though F2 onset changes in equal acoustic steps.","children":[]},{"text":"Discrimination is better near the /b/–/d/ and /d/–/g/ boundaries than within the stretches receiving one label. The one-step, two-step, and three-step comparisons all relate sensitivity to the location of those boundaries. Chance performance is about **50% correct** in these discrimination tasks.","children":[]},{"text":"Wider physical separation generally improves performance, but the category relationship also matters. Sounds assigned to different phonemes are easier to distinguish than similarly spaced sounds assigned to the same phoneme.","children":[]},{"text":"Predictions based on the labeling responses capture the boundary-related peaks in discrimination. Listeners retain some acoustic sensitivity beyond the simplest categorical prediction, so identification and discrimination are closely related without being identical measures.","children":[]}],"review-6":[{"text":"The **number of looks to the other member of the minimal pair increases gradually as you approach the category boundary**, even when those stimuli receive the same final phoneme label. A **cascaded account** preserves the partial evidence that varies across those tokens and passes it into word recognition.","children":[]},{"text":"A strict threshold account discards those distinctions when every token produces the same completed phoneme output. The later word level would then receive the same information and lack the changing input needed to explain the graded competitor effect.","children":[]}],"review-7":[{"text":"**Feedback from the lexical layer to the phoneme layer** allows activated word representations to send activity back toward the phonemes that form those words. A partly matching sound activates a candidate word, and that word increases support for the phonemes compatible with its form.","children":[]},{"text":"The interaction is reciprocal because phonetic evidence affects lexical activation, and lexical activation changes the balance among phoneme interpretations. Forward cascading and backward feedback are separate properties, although both can operate while a word is being recognized.","children":[]}],"review-8":[{"text":"A **late correction process** leaves categorical perception functioning as usual but introduces a **bias to report having heard a word** after the initial phoneme decision. For corpse/gorpse, the response process favors the interpretation that produces corpse.","children":[]},{"text":"Under this account, word knowledge changes the report after phoneme identification. A general word-reporting bias is predicted across the continuum, including positions away from the most ambiguous category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"The **word–nonword**, **neutral**, and **nonword–word** conditions differ in which phoneme response creates a real word. The late-correction prediction shifts those responses without requiring lexical knowledge to change the earlier phoneme categorization itself.","children":[]}],"review-9":[{"text":"If **word knowledge** works to **shift the categorical boundary**, the location where listeners switch between phoneme labels changes toward an interpretation that forms a real word. The same ambiguous sound can receive different phoneme interpretations depending on which interpretation produces a real word.","children":[]},{"text":"The lexical effect is strongest **closest to the category boundary**. At the endpoints, the acoustic evidence strongly supports one consonant, whereas near the boundary lexical information can resolve uncertainty about the initial consonant.","children":[]},{"text":"The predicted difference between lexical conditions therefore rises near the ambiguous region and falls toward the endpoints. It differs from a general reporting bias that remains similar across continuum positions.","children":[]}],"review-10":[{"text":"Ganong’s results are consistent with **shifts** in phoneme labeling, with the largest word-related difference near the category boundary rather than an equal correction across the whole continuum. The labeling functions shift according to which consonant produces a real word, with smaller differences toward the clearer endpoints.","children":[]},{"text":"That pattern is consistent with a change in the phoneme boundary rather than a uniform word-reporting bias. Ganong considered guessing strategies, while interactive models implemented the effect through **feedback from lexical representations to phoneme representations**.","children":[]}],"review-11":[{"text":"In a **simulation of the Ganong effect in the TRACE model**, the initial input is ambiguous between **/b/** and **/p/** before **/l/**, the vowel in plug, and the final **/g/** supply more information over time. Both possible onsets initially receive activation because the first acoustic information supports more than one interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"As the following segments arrive, compatible words become active. Early /b/ support can contribute to candidates beginning with bl-, while /p/ support contributes to candidates beginning with pl-. Activation changes as each candidate is matched against more of the input.","children":[]},{"text":"Before the ending is complete, **plug, blood, and blush** can all receive support from partial matches. The final /g/ strongly favors plug over the competing interpretation of the initial consonant. Activation for the compatible word increases while the alternatives lose support, and feedback increases the phoneme activity consistent with that word.","children":[]}],"review-12":[{"text":"As **input unfolds over time**, activity accumulates for **plug**, especially when the **final /g/** arrives and conflicts with **blood** and **blush**, which can also be partly active. As the input unfolds, plug accumulates more activation than the candidates that were temporarily compatible with the beginning.","children":[]},{"text":"Increased activation for plug feeds back to the phoneme level and strengthens **/p/**, the onset consistent with the whole word. Later information can therefore change the balance between interpretations of an earlier ambiguous sound.","children":[]},{"text":"This **top-down processing** within TRACE allows word-level activity to influence phoneme-level activity. The lexical context contributes to the category boundary through the model’s connections rather than being applied only as a final reporting correction.","children":[]}],"review-13":[{"text":"A **parser** constructs a **syntactic tree for the sentences we hear or read**, identifying which words belong together and how phrases relate within the sentence. It operates with **limited memory** and **time constraints**, so representing every possible interpretation in full would place demands on the cognitive system.","children":[]},{"text":"The reduced-relative analysis contains an embedded clause inside the noun phrase. The horse is modified by the equivalent of **that was raced past the barn**, and **fell** is the main verb. The simpler initial analysis instead treats **raced past the barn** as the main verb phrase.","children":[]},{"text":"A **noun phrase (NP)** groups a noun with its associated material, a **verb phrase (VP)** groups the verb with its complements, and a **prepositional phrase (PP)** includes a preposition and its object. Assigning these relationships determines how the words contribute to the sentence rather than merely identifying their individual meanings.","children":[]}],"review-14":[{"text":"**Minimal attachment** is a strategy to **initially prefer smaller trees**, choosing the syntactic analysis with fewer nodes rather than building every possible structure at once. A node represents a constituent or grammatical grouping, so a smaller tree uses fewer such groupings.","children":[]},{"text":"Treating **raced** as the main verb yields a relatively simple analysis. Treating **raced past the barn** as a reduced relative requires an embedded clause within the noun phrase, while **fell** remains outside it as the main predicate.","children":[]},{"text":"The smaller-tree preference reduces the initial structural commitment in memory. It can also produce a garden path when the simpler analysis is incompatible with the eventual continuation.","children":[]}],"review-15":[{"text":"**Late closure** is a **bias to keep the current clause open as long as possible**, attaching incoming material to the clause or phrase already being processed. In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the dynamite,”** **with the dynamite** is preferentially associated with **blew open**.","children":[]},{"text":"Under that attachment, the dynamite is the instrument used in opening the safe. An alternative attachment places the prepositional phrase inside **the safe with the dynamite**, making it describe the safe instead.","children":[]},{"text":"The words support both structural possibilities, but the parser is biased toward the verb-phrase attachment. The structural decision determines whether the phrase specifies an instrument of the action or a property of the object.","children":[]}],"review-16":[{"text":"In **“The burglar blew open the safe with the jewels,”** the initial verb-phrase attachment momentarily treats **the jewels as the material used to blow open the safe**, although that interpretation is implausible. The sensible interpretation instead groups **with the jewels** with **the safe**, identifying the safe containing them.","children":[]},{"text":"Reading times and eye movements indicate slower processing when the sensible interpretation requires the **unpreferred attachment**. The parser initially adopts one relationship and then revises it when the resulting event interpretation is unsuitable.","children":[]}],"review-17":[{"text":"An autonomous account proposes a **parser that deals exclusively with the abstract syntactic structure of the sentence**, applying its own operating preferences rather than using meaning to choose the initial structure. **Minimal attachment** favors smaller trees, while **late closure** favors attaching new material to the current structure.","children":[]},{"text":"The account initially handles ambiguity by committing to a preferred parse instead of fully activating every possible structure. Revision becomes necessary when the continuation cannot be incorporated into that analysis.","children":[]},{"text":"In this proposal, meaning does not determine the earliest structural decision. Difficulty arises when a structurally preferred interpretation must later be replaced by an alternative compatible with the sentence.","children":[]}],"review-18":[{"text":"**World knowledge can be very local**, as in **Spivey and colleagues’ task (1995)**, where a visual world contained **a pencil, a box, a towel and an apple on a towel** while participants followed a spoken instruction. Participants heard **“Put the apple on the towel in the box”** and prepared the corresponding movement.","children":[]},{"text":"**On the towel** can temporarily be interpreted as the destination of put, making the empty towel a possible target. In the intended interpretation it identifies the apple’s current location, while **in the box** specifies the destination.","children":[]},{"text":"Participants often looked at the empty towel before shifting to the box. The **unambiguous** instruction **“Put the apple that’s on the towel in the box”** explicitly marks the location phrase as a modifier and produces less temporary consideration of the towel as a destination.","children":[]}],"review-19":[{"text":"**World knowledge can be very local** in a two-referent context, where one apple is on a towel and another is on a different support, creating two possible objects for the same noun. The phrase **the apple on the towel** specifies which apple should be moved.","children":[]},{"text":"Eye movements pass from the starting fixation to the target apple and then the box, rather than making the same detour toward an incorrect towel destination. The sequence and timing are similar for the ambiguous instruction and the explicitly relative-clause version.","children":[]},{"text":"A modifier identifying the apple is useful when more than one apple is available. The local referential context therefore favors interpreting **on the towel** as part of the noun phrase.","children":[]}],"review-20":[{"text":"With **one apple in the display**, the instruction makes the empty towel a tempting but incorrect destination, while having **two apples in the display** changes how the same ambiguous words can identify the intended object. With **two apples**, participants make substantially fewer such looks.","children":[]},{"text":"The explicitly unambiguous instruction produces relatively few incorrect-destination looks in either context. The major ambiguity cost is therefore concentrated in the one-referent condition.","children":[]},{"text":"Two possible apples make additional identifying information appropriate. The phrase **on the towel** can select the intended apple, reducing the temporary interpretation of towel as the movement goal.","children":[]}],"review-21":[{"text":"**Wernicke’s aphasia** is described as **word salad or Jabberwocky, often with neologisms**, because fluent output can contain inappropriate word choices or invented forms that make the intended meaning difficult to recover. A patient reporting poor vision said, **“My wires don’t hire right.”** The subject–verb structure is recognizable, but the selected words do not communicate the intended visual complaint.","children":[]},{"text":"Another patient answered a question about health with **“I felt worse because I can no longer keep in mind from the mind of the minds…”** Familiar phrase structures continue while the message loses coherence. An aphasic physician used **“demaploze”** and **“tubaboys,”** invented forms placed within grammatical-sounding phrases.","children":[]},{"text":"Object descriptions could also be fluent but inappropriate. A fork was **“a need for a schedule,”** and a spoon was **“How many schemes on your throat.”** Words are being produced, but their selection does not produce an adequate description of the object.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** can produce **telegraphic speech**, with relative preservation of important content words and difficulty with grammatical particles and word order. A patient’s hospital account included **“Monday,” “Dad,” “hospital,” “two doctors,” “thirty minutes,” “Wednesday,” “nine o’clock,” “Thursday,” “ten o’clock,”** and **“teeth.”** Pauses and missing grammatical connections make the account halting, although the surviving content conveys more of the intended event than the fluent Wernicke-type examples.","children":[]}],"review-22":[{"text":"A traditional distinction describes **Wernicke’s aphasia as problems with activation or inhibition in the lexicon**, while **Broca’s aphasia involves problems with grammar**, separating difficulties selecting words from difficulties organizing grammatical relations. **Activation** makes a word available, while **inhibition** suppresses competitors that should not be selected.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s original patient Leborgne**, nicknamed **Tan**, could produce essentially one syllable but appeared to retain other abilities and to understand what was said. His inability to articulate speech was different from the agrammatic but partly interpretable output associated with the later syndrome.","children":[]},{"text":"The original cases may be better described in terms of **apraxia of speech**, a difficulty organizing speech movements. A motor-production impairment and an impairment of grammatical knowledge are different proposed causes of limited speech output.","children":[]}],"review-23":[{"text":"A **sharp distinction between the grammar and the lexicon** separates the abstract rules that combine words into sentences from the stored word representations available for those combinations. In the formal linguistic account, understanding a new sentence involves the grammatical process that generates its structure, rather than matching it to an already memorized sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"**“Colorless green ideas sleep furiously”** is grammatical despite its unusual meaning. **“Furiously sleep ideas green colorless”** contains the same words but lacks the normal grammatical organization. Familiarity with a meaningful event cannot by itself explain the difference.","children":[]},{"text":"The proposed aphasia division assigns disrupted lexical activation to Wernicke’s aphasia and disrupted grammatical structure to Broca’s aphasia. This is a hypothesis about separable processes, beyond the descriptive contrast between fluent and nonfluent speech.","children":[]}],"review-24":[{"text":"In conversation, **there are lots of redundant cues to what a speaker intends**, so **“The dog bit the man”** can be understood through word meanings, event plausibility, ordinary word order, and discourse context. The words DOG, BITE, and MAN support a familiar event, and English canonical **subject–verb–object** order also supports interpreting the first noun as the agent. Conversation often adds a discourse context that further clarifies the intended event.","children":[]},{"text":"A **heuristic** is a simplifying strategy that substitutes a regular pattern for a fuller analysis of the sentence. A listener relying on likely events or the first-noun-as-agent pattern could reach the right interpretation without successfully analyzing every grammatical relationship.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The dog that the man is biting is angry”** assigns the biting action to the man. The dog is being bitten, although it is the first noun and dogs are more typical biters. Recovering the intended meaning requires separating the main-clause subject from the agent inside the relative clause.","children":[]}],"review-25":[{"text":"**Caramazza and Zurif devised a list of sentences and a set of control sentences** with straightforward actions, including **“The girl is kicking a green ball,” “The cat is chasing a blue bird,” “The girl is pulling a red wagon,”** and **“The man is reading a yellow book.”** The remaining controls describe a boy riding a new bicycle, a girl holding a broken doll, a boy throwing a round ball, and a boy carrying a sharp pencil. The agent normally occurs first, and familiar event knowledge supports the interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"In **semantically constrained center-embedded sentences**, one clause occurs inside another but likely event roles strongly support who acts on what. The materials include **“The apple that the boy is eating is red,” “The wagon that the horse is pulling is green,” “The book that the girl is reading is yellow,”** and **“The bat that the boy is carrying is brown.”** Other items concern a boy holding a bicycle, a girl wearing a coat, a woman washing a dress, and a clown holding a balloon.","children":[]},{"text":"In **reversible center-embedded sentences**, either participant could plausibly perform the action. The materials include **“The cow that the monkey is scaring is yellow,” “The horse that the bear is kicking is brown,” “The cat that the dog is biting is black,”** and **“The fish that the frog is biting is green.”** Other pairs involve a tiger chasing a lion, a boy hitting a girl, a woman hugging a man, and a boy kicking a girl. Grammar specifies which participant is the agent because exchanging the roles still produces a possible event.","children":[]},{"text":"**Improbable center-embedded sentences** make ordinary event expectations favor the wrong direction. The items include **“The horse that the bird is kicking is brown,” “The lion that the baby is scaring is yellow,” “The bird that the worm is eating is blue,”** and **“The dog that the man is biting is black.”** Other items assign riding to a horse acting on a man, spanking to a boy acting on a woman, chasing to a robber acting on a policeman, and patting to a cat acting on a girl. The embedded-clause noun performs the action even when that relationship is unusual.","children":[]}],"review-26":[{"text":"In **a sentence-picture matching task**, healthy controls are **more or less equally good at all stimulus types**, including control, semantically constrained, reversible, and improbable sentences that place different demands on interpreting roles. Their accuracy changes little when likely event roles stop supporting the grammatical interpretation.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke’s patients** perform poorly overall, consistent with broader comprehension difficulties. **Broca’s patients** have greater difficulty when they must keep track of who did what to whom without relying on familiar semantic roles.","children":[]},{"text":"The Broca group performs better when plausibility supports the grammatical relationship and worse for reversible or improbable materials. Caramazza and Zurif interpreted this selective comprehension difficulty as evidence for a syntactic impairment, beyond the patients’ difficulties producing speech.","children":[]}],"review-27":[{"text":"In **Dick and colleagues’ task**, participants listen to a sentence and **indicate which animal is doing the action**, identifying the agent rather than simply reporting which animal names they heard. The task holds the event constant while changing its grammatical form.","children":[]},{"text":"**“The dog is biting the cat”** is an **active** sentence, and **“It’s the dog that is biting the cat”** is a **subject cleft**. Both place the dog, the agent, before the cat.","children":[]},{"text":"**“It’s the cat that the dog is biting”** is an **object cleft**, and **“The cat is bitten by the dog”** is a **passive**. Both begin with the cat, the participant receiving the action. Correctly identifying the actor requires the grammatical relationship rather than simply choosing the first noun.","children":[]}],"review-28":[{"text":"When **aphasia patients and healthy aging controls** interpret active, subject-cleft, object-cleft, and passive sentences, healthy controls perform accurately across the forms while patient groups differ in their comprehension patterns. Patients with **anomic aphasia** perform better overall than several other aphasia groups but still lose accuracy for the harder forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Broca’s aphasia** and **conduction aphasia** are associated with a marked decline for object clefts and lower performance for passives than the easiest forms. **Wernicke’s patients** have poor accuracy across all four forms.","children":[]},{"text":"Broca’s patients have particular difficulty with noncanonical structures, but their active-sentence performance is also below that of healthy controls. The impairment combines a general reduction in performance with additional vulnerability when the grammatical arrangement is harder.","children":[]}],"review-29":[{"text":"**Healthy undergraduates under conditions that stress the language system** can also lose comprehension accuracy, even though they have no aphasia, when speech or competing task demands make processing more difficult. The manipulations include compressed or faster speech, low-pass filtering, noise, and remembering auditory or visual digit lists during the sentence task.","children":[]},{"text":"**Object-cleft and passive sentences are particularly vulnerable to all manipulations**, while active sentences and subject clefts remain relatively easier. **Combined stress conditions**, including low-pass filtering with compression, produce larger losses than the normal listening condition.","children":[]},{"text":"These listeners have no aphasic brain injury, yet the harder structures produce a disproportionate deficit when processing is taxed. The corresponding pattern in aphasia can therefore arise partly from a **performance limitation**, rather than uniquely identifying loss of a grammatical rule.","children":[]}],"review-30":[{"text":"**Saygin and colleagues (2004)** asked patients to **match the action to the object**, identifying which object fits the meaning of an action presented either linguistically or without spoken language. In a nonverbal condition, a pictured action such as licking must be related to an object such as an ice cream rather than an unrelated choice. A linguistic condition conveys the relevant information with language.","children":[]},{"text":"Aphasic participants were less accurate in both conditions. Accuracy was about **93.4%** for nonlinguistic items and **87.5%** for linguistic items, compared with **98.5%** and **99.4%** for controls. Broca-type comprehension difficulties therefore extended to interpreting actions without words.","children":[]},{"text":"Semantically related distractors were more disruptive than unrelated choices. An incorrect object that is related in meaning competes more strongly with the intended action–object match.","children":[]}],"review-31":[{"text":"**Performance in the non-verbal version of the task was significantly correlated with damage to Broca’s area**, linking poor action matching with injury in that region even when spoken sentence processing was absent. The affected behavior concerned interpreting pantomimed action rather than producing a spoken sentence.","children":[]},{"text":"The association links frontal damage with a comprehension demand outside language. A deficit tied to that region can therefore involve action interpretation as well as the grammatical difficulties measured with verbal materials.","children":[]}],"review-32":[{"text":"In the **conditioned head turn procedure**, an infant’s response to a detected sound change becomes measurable through an orienting head movement, rather than through a spoken judgment. The infant learns that an auditory event predicts a rewarding visual event and turns toward it.","children":[]},{"text":"**High-amplitude sucking** uses sucking as the response measure. Repeated sound exposure can reduce responding, and a renewed response after a sound change indicates discrimination. Neither method requires the infant to pronounce the sound or report a verbal label.","children":[]}],"review-33":[{"text":"**Categorical perception in infants** was tested by **Eimas and colleagues (1971)** through controlled differences in **voice onset time**, the delay between releasing a stop consonant and beginning vocal-fold vibration. Sucking declined during repeated exposure before a sound change was introduced.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **20D** condition, the sound changed by **20 milliseconds** across different adult phoneme categories, from **+20 to +40 milliseconds**. Sucking increased again when the stimulus changed across the category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **20S** condition, the physical change was also 20 milliseconds, but the pair remained within one adult category, with values of **−20 to 0** or **+60 to +80 milliseconds**. The equally large change within a category produced much less recovery in sucking. In the **0** control, the sound stayed the same and responding continued to decline.","children":[]},{"text":"The boundary-crossing and within-category changes were physically matched. The stronger response to the boundary-crossing change indicates that the infant’s sensitivity was organized in relation to a phonetic category distinction rather than the size of the timing difference alone.","children":[]}],"review-34":[{"text":"Categorical perception was considered a **first step in turning speech into a symbolic format that could be used by linguistic processes**, converting variable acoustic input into a representation suited to language. Its presence in very young infants was therefore taken as possible evidence for an innate speech-processing ability.","children":[]},{"text":"At about **four months**, infants are developing gross motor abilities such as manipulating objects with two hands and holding the head and chest up while lying prone. Color vision and general visual acuity are also still developing.","children":[]},{"text":"Vocal production is only beginning to include more vowel-like sounds rather than creaky whines. Category-related perception at this age precedes mature speech production, which contributed to the interpretation that the perceptual organization was available very early.","children":[]}],"review-35":[{"text":"Through **perceptual narrowing for speech categories**, infants’ ability to discriminate some nonnative speech contrasts declines over development, even when those contrasts were distinguishable earlier in infancy. Around six months, English-learning infants can discriminate some contrasts they have not encountered in their own language.","children":[]},{"text":"In the **Werker and Tees** comparison, discrimination of tested **Hindi** and **Salish** contrasts is strong at **6–8 months**, lower at **8–10 months**, and much lower at **10–12 months**. Relevant Hindi or Salish experience is associated with stronger continuing performance.","children":[]},{"text":"Native-language development therefore changes which differences remain perceptually available. A phonetic distinction that is detectable early can become harder to perceive as the system becomes adapted to the language heard regularly.","children":[]}],"review-36":[{"text":"Under the **citizens of the world** account, categorical perception is a special property of the language system that provides **innate, language-general knowledge about speech**, rather than depending entirely on learning particular contrasts. Early categorical-perception abilities are treated as a special property of the language system.","children":[]},{"text":"Under this account, learning **selects among innately specified phonetic contrasts**. Experience narrows a broad initial inventory to distinctions that matter in the child’s native language. The developmental change is primarily selection and tuning, rather than construction of all categories from an initially undifferentiated signal.","children":[]}],"review-37":[{"text":"The **inductive problem of early word learning** arises because the same encounter with a cat supports many possible meanings, including an animal category, an individual, an action, a property, or a part. A novel word near a cat could name the category **CAT**, the broader category **ANIMAL**, the role **PET**, or the individual **FELIX**.","children":[]},{"text":"It could instead describe **FURRY**, **ALIVE**, or **WALKING**, or refer to **WHISKERS** or a **LEG**. The same visible animal provides a whole object, properties, parts, actions, and broader or narrower classifications.","children":[]},{"text":"The **hypothesis space** is the set of candidate meanings compatible with that experience. Quine’s **gavagai problem** concerns this uncertainty about reference, because pairing an unfamiliar word with a scene does not uniquely determine what the word means.","children":[]}],"review-38":[{"text":"**Joint attention** involves **sharing an attentional focus with another**, allowing a child and another person to coordinate which object or event their interaction concerns. The child and adult attend to the same object and coordinate that focus through social information.","children":[]},{"text":"**Gaze following** emerges around **nine months**, allowing infants to follow the direction of another person’s eyes. Following another person’s gaze supports identifying what that person is referring to, rather than assuming every spoken label concerns whichever object is already most salient to the child.","children":[]}],"review-39":[{"text":"In **Baldwin and colleagues’ joint attention group**, an **adult interacting with the baby labeled the object the baby was playing with**, coordinating the spoken label with a visibly shared focus. In the **no-joint-attention condition**, a voice behind a screen labeled that object without the visible shared interaction.","children":[]},{"text":"**Eighteen-month-olds** learned the novel word in the joint-attention condition but not in the no-joint-attention condition. An object and a spoken label occurred in both, but learning differed with the social coordination of reference.","children":[]}],"review-40":[{"text":"**Intentional cues from the speaker**, such as **eye gaze direction**, help children narrow the hypothesis space for a new word by identifying what the speaker is trying to refer to. The speaker can be referring to an object different from the one the infant is currently handling.","children":[]},{"text":"Baldwin tested **16–17-month-olds** and **18–19-month-olds** with two labeling arrangements. In **follow-in labeling**, the adult looked at the toy already occupying the infant’s attention. In **discrepant labeling**, the adult looked at a toy in a bucket instead.","children":[]},{"text":"The contrast separates the child’s own current focus from the adult’s intended referent. Tracking gaze provides information about which object the new label is meant to name.","children":[]}],"review-41":[{"text":"**Intentional cues from the speaker** include **affective and behavioral cues**, so a person’s facial expression and actions can provide information about what a new label refers to. A facial expression can distinguish an object that satisfies the adult’s goal from one that is not the intended target.","children":[]},{"text":"In **Akhtar and colleagues’ study**, infants used adults’ facial expressions to identify the intended referent of a novel word. Word learning therefore incorporated social evidence about the adult’s response to an object, rather than only the sound’s temporal association with everything present.","children":[]}],"review-42":[{"text":"**Default strategies that children bring to the word learning situation** constrain the set of possible meanings considered when they hear an unfamiliar word used to name something. A default favors one interpretation unless other evidence supports a different one, reducing the number of candidate meanings considered.","children":[]},{"text":"The **whole-object assumption** favors interpreting a new label as the name of an entire object rather than an isolated part, property, or action. This narrows the hypothesis space without requiring every visible aspect of the scene to be tested separately.","children":[]}],"review-43":[{"text":"Under **mutual exclusivity**, associated with **Markman (1989)**, **as a default, objects can have only one name**, so an already known label influences where a new label is assigned. If a child already knows a label for one object, a novel label is more likely to be assigned to an object without a known name.","children":[]},{"text":"This strategy emerges during the **second half of the second year**. Existing word knowledge constrains new reference because an already named object becomes a less likely candidate for the unfamiliar word.","children":[]}],"review-44":[{"text":"**Lenneberg (1967)** proposed that the sensitive period has to do with **the development of cerebral dominance, or developmental changes in language laterality**, linking favorable conditions for language acquisition with increasing hemispheric specialization. Developmental changes in language laterality were connected with changes in the capacity to acquire language.","children":[]},{"text":"The proposal combined information about neural development and language milestones with the greater resilience of children after brain injury or surgery. Age-related recovery differences supplied evidence that the language system’s organization changes across development.","children":[]}],"review-45":[{"text":"Much of what is known about **L1 sensitive periods** comes from **idiosyncratic cases or relatively rare phenomena**, including rare injuries or unusual histories of language deprivation. Those cases combine language timing with other developmental circumstances.","children":[]},{"text":"**Second-language learning** occurs at many ages in a much larger population. Variation in eventual L2 success can therefore be compared with the age at which learning began, providing a different source of evidence about developmental constraints.","children":[]}],"review-46":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport** tested the English proficiency attained by **46 native Korean or Chinese speakers** who had arrived in the United States between **ages three and thirty-nine**, comparing the long-term outcomes of learners with different arrival ages. At testing, they had lived there for **three to twenty-six years**.","children":[]},{"text":"The participants completed a **grammaticality judgment task** covering several English structures. The study compared eventual performance with age of arrival rather than measuring only the speed of the first weeks of instruction.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier arrivals generally performed more accurately, with a strong age relationship before puberty. Later arrivals performed less accurately overall but varied considerably, and their scores were not tightly ordered by their arrival ages.","children":[]},{"text":"The reported age effect was not accounted for by the measured differences in English experience, motivation, self-consciousness, or American identification. Johnson and Newport interpreted the pattern as evidence that maturational state influences L2 acquisition.","children":[]}],"review-47":[{"text":"**Early learners up to about 8 are often near-perfect (native-like)**, achieving very high grammaticality accuracy when their arrival and substantial English exposure begin during early childhood. Among later childhood arrivals, increasing AOA is associated with lower accuracy.","children":[]},{"text":"Adult-arrival scores vary substantially without a comparably strong ordering by age. The **nearly random distribution** describes the weak relationship between adult arrival age and individual accuracy, rather than claiming that every late learner answers each question randomly.","children":[]}],"review-48":[{"text":"Under **the exercise hypothesis**, **early in life humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages**, and that capacity disappears or declines with maturation if it is not exercised during the favorable period. If exercised during the favorable period, that capacity remains available for additional language learning throughout life.","children":[]},{"text":"The **maturational-state hypothesis** also proposes an early superior capacity, but its decline follows maturation even when language was learned successfully during childhood. Early use does not permanently preserve the same learning state.","children":[]},{"text":"The hypotheses differ in what they predict for a person who already has an early L1. Under exercise, prior use protects later language learning, whereas under the maturational-state account, an established L1 can coexist with reduced capacity for learning a later language.","children":[]}],"review-49":[{"text":"The **cohort effect** involves competition from a word that shares the beginning of a spoken target, so several words can receive activation before the later sounds distinguish them. In the **Allopenna** task, **beaker** and **beetle** share an onset, **speaker** shares the ending with beaker, and **carriage** is unrelated.","children":[]},{"text":"Target and cohort looks increase early, before the continuing input differentiates their forms. The target then gains more looking while cohort looking declines, and rhyme competition follows a different time course.","children":[]},{"text":"Competition is therefore sensitive to when overlapping information occurs within a spoken word. The final target response can conceal alternatives that were active earlier during the same recognition event.","children":[]}],"review-50":[{"text":"**Marian and Spivey examined the cohort effect with cross-language competitors**, testing whether Russian-English bilinguals consider objects whose names overlap with the target in the other language. The Russian word **спички, spichki**, meaning matches, begins similarly to English **speaker**. During an English instruction naming speaker, the matches object can therefore receive support from its Russian name.","children":[]},{"text":"The English within-language competitor **spear** shares the target’s onset as well. Displays can include the within-language competitor, the Russian-name competitor, or both, separating the sources of overlap.","children":[]},{"text":"Other cross-language target relations include **boot** with Russian **buben**, a tambourine, **chair** with **cherepaha**, a turtle, and **marker** with **marka**, a stamp. A look toward such an object during English processing indicates activation tied to the other language’s form rather than its English label.","children":[]}],"review-51":[{"text":"During **L2 spoken word recognition**, **when only one competitor was present, participants made eye movements to both within- and between-language competitors to the same extent**, about **18% of trials** for each competitor type. Both within-language and between-language overlap therefore affected which objects participants looked at during recognition.","children":[]},{"text":"When both competitors were present, within-language looks occurred on about **19%** of trials and between-language looks on about **13%**. The control filler attracted looks on about **7%** of trials.","children":[]},{"text":"Within-language competition was stronger when the alternatives competed together, but the L1-name competitor still attracted substantially more looking than an unrelated control. Activation during L2 recognition therefore extended across language membership.","children":[]}],"review-52":[{"text":"During **L1 spoken word recognition in Russian**, a **within-language competitor** attracted looks on about **14% of trials when alone** and **13% when both competitors were present**, exceeding looks to unrelated control objects. Between-language English competitors attracted about **8%**, compared with a control rate near **5%**.","children":[]},{"text":"The small difference between the English-name competitor and control was not statistically significant. The reported cross-language effect was therefore asymmetric, with clearer L1 competition during L2 processing than L2 competition during L1 processing.","children":[]}],"review-53":[{"text":"**The Revised Hierarchical Model of Kroll and Stewart** explains **L1-L2 interactions in terms of spreading activation**, linking the two lexical systems with shared concepts through connections of unequal strength. A lexical link joins word representations, while a conceptual link connects a word with meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"The **L1 lexicon** is proposed to be larger and more strongly connected to concepts. The developing **L2 lexicon** has stronger lexical connections toward L1 than L1 has toward L2, reflecting the translation direction emphasized during learning.","children":[]},{"text":"Activation therefore spreads more readily from an L2 word to its L1 counterpart than in the reverse direction. Unequal link strengths produce unequal interaction even when both languages belong to the same person.","children":[]}],"review-54":[{"text":"The **Revised Hierarchical Model** explains **L1-L2 interactions in terms of spreading activation**, allowing activity from an L2 word to reach the L1 lexicon and feed back to conceptual representations through links of unequal strength. During the **Marian and Spivey** task in English, the competing Russian word for **matches** becomes partly active and can activate the concept associated with the matches object.","children":[]},{"text":"The English target **speaker** and the Russian word **spichki**, meaning matches, overlap in their initial sounds. The competition concerns those word forms rather than a similarity in the meanings of speaker and matches. Activation of the Russian competitor can therefore draw the eyes toward the matches object even though its English name does not resemble speaker.","children":[]},{"text":"During recognition in **L1**, the weaker links leading to L2 make the reverse interference less reliable. The model connects the different eye-movement patterns with unequal strengths of activation between the two lexicons and their conceptual representations.","children":[]}],"review-55":[{"text":"**Balanced bilinguals do not look like two monolinguals in one**, because similarly strong conceptual links can keep **competitive dynamics and inhibition** engaged across both languages during recognition. Equal proficiency does not require the languages to function as isolated systems.","children":[]},{"text":"The prediction differs from treating a bilingual as **“two monolinguals in one.”** Activation can spread across shared concepts and both lexical systems, creating control demands even when the intended response is in only one language.","children":[]},{"text":"This interaction motivated research on performance in **nonlinguistic cognitive-control tasks**. The model’s immediate claim concerns the competition and inhibition produced by its connected language representations.","children":[]}]}};
